INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$0ed8f24f$SATQ$, $SATQ$f118bde5-9bc4-487f-868c-d6d9f2e1af8e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>K.D. Leka and colleagues found that the Sun&rsquo;s corona provides an advance indication of solar flares&mdash;intense eruptions of electromagnetic radiation that emanate from active regions in the Sun&rsquo;s photosphere and can interfere with telecommunications on Earth. Preceding a flare, the corona temporarily exhibits increased brightness above the region where the flare is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ac7931a5-aac6-4a46-89ed-ddf1c40ab854","content":"<p>antecedent</p>"},{"id":"018f8a7e-dbb0-4746-96f9-3584f53664bb","content":"<p>impending</p>"},{"id":"21bd34f8-5372-4d4c-ae2b-aaef7994ba00","content":"<p>innocuous</p>"},{"id":"2594b791-5d30-4a3c-bb1c-538c2a4e9c5e","content":"<p>perpetual</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["018f8a7e-dbb0-4746-96f9-3584f53664bb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of advance indications of solar flares. In this context the word "impending" means imminent or approaching. The text mentions a study by Leka and colleagues that found that the Sun&rsquo;s corona provides an advance indication of solar flares. The text then points out why such an advance indication would be useful&mdash;solar flares can interfere with communications on Earth&mdash;and concludes by describing the characteristic of the corona that gives warning of a solar flare. The text indicates that this characteristic&mdash;increased brightness in a particular region of the corona&mdash;comes before the appearance of the flare. Therefore, in context, the best answer would indicate that the flare is approaching, or impending.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The best answer would be one that indicates that the increased brightness of the Sun&rsquo;s corona precedes the appearance of the flare. But if the flare were "antecedent," or previous, then the flare would instead precede the appearance of the increased brightness of the corona, a statement that is logically inconsistent. Choice C is incorrect. The word "innocuous," or harmless, does not logically complete the text; since solar flares can interfere with communications on Earth, they cannot reasonably be described as innocuous. Choice D is incorrect. If the solar flares have an advance indication of their appearance, then there must therefore be a time before the appearance of the flares when they do not exist. But the word "perpetual," or never-ending, would in context indicate that the flare exists at the same time as the advance indication provided by the Sun&rsquo;s corona, which would not make logical sense.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0ed8f24f","external_id":"f118bde5-9bc4-487f-868c-d6d9f2e1af8e","vaultid":null,"uId":"f495bc98-4c9a-4323-9af2-712cbb1d407b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>K.D. Leka and colleagues found that the Sun&rsquo;s corona provides an advance indication of solar flares&mdash;intense eruptions of electromagnetic radiation that emanate from active regions in the Sun&rsquo;s photosphere and can interfere with telecommunications on Earth. Preceding a flare, the corona temporarily exhibits increased brightness above the region where the flare is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ac7931a5-aac6-4a46-89ed-ddf1c40ab854","content":"<p>antecedent</p>"},{"id":"018f8a7e-dbb0-4746-96f9-3584f53664bb","content":"<p>impending</p>"},{"id":"21bd34f8-5372-4d4c-ae2b-aaef7994ba00","content":"<p>innocuous</p>"},{"id":"2594b791-5d30-4a3c-bb1c-538c2a4e9c5e","content":"<p>perpetual</p>"}],"keys":["018f8a7e-dbb0-4746-96f9-3584f53664bb"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of advance indications of solar flares. In this context the word \"impending\" means imminent or approaching. The text mentions a study by Leka and colleagues that found that the Sun&rsquo;s corona provides an advance indication of solar flares. The text then points out why such an advance indication would be useful&mdash;solar flares can interfere with communications on Earth&mdash;and concludes by describing the characteristic of the corona that gives warning of a solar flare. The text indicates that this characteristic&mdash;increased brightness in a particular region of the corona&mdash;comes before the appearance of the flare. Therefore, in context, the best answer would indicate that the flare is approaching, or impending.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The best answer would be one that indicates that the increased brightness of the Sun&rsquo;s corona precedes the appearance of the flare. But if the flare were \"antecedent,\" or previous, then the flare would instead precede the appearance of the increased brightness of the corona, a statement that is logically inconsistent. Choice C is incorrect. The word \"innocuous,\" or harmless, does not logically complete the text; since solar flares can interfere with communications on Earth, they cannot reasonably be described as innocuous. Choice D is incorrect. If the solar flares have an advance indication of their appearance, then there must therefore be a time before the appearance of the flares when they do not exist. But the word \"perpetual,\" or never-ending, would in context indicate that the flare exists at the same time as the advance indication provided by the Sun&rsquo;s corona, which would not make logical sense.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323052,"updateDate":1730147323052}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323052, 1730147323052),
    ($SATQ$0ed94d4c$SATQ$, $SATQ$fa3b0868-3f29-4a0e-abca-9354255b5f4a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$238db56f-cc1d-42d9-80c0-27d1c1e3f154$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jackie Ormes&rsquo;s <em>Torchy Brown in Dixie to Harlem</em> (1937&ndash;38) was the first comic strip by a Black woman to appear in a widely read newspaper. The strip tells the story of Torchy, a young woman who leaves Mississippi to become a performer in New York City. Torchy&rsquo;s story reflects the experience of the Great Migration (1910&ndash;1970), when millions of Black Americans left the South in search of opportunities in other parts of the United States. <em>Torchy Brown</em> thus shows how Ormes used comics to comment humorously on issues affecting Black Americans, which she continued to do throughout her career.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c2f91fa1-8abf-43e3-bec9-d5169d94a871","content":"<p>To show how Ormes&rsquo;s <em>Torchy Brown</em> inspired other Black women to write comic strips in the 1930s</p>"},{"id":"ccae3f88-31a5-49be-abdd-be429b1f9032","content":"<p>To illustrate how the subjects Ormes addressed in her comic strips changed over the course of her career</p>"},{"id":"98caec36-6c34-40cd-80b3-395002111565","content":"<p>To give an example of how Ormes presented the experiences of Black Americans in her comic strips&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e98c3561-4a43-4ab6-a35f-1fddf756c3bf","content":"<p>To claim that several characters in <em>Torchy Brown</em> were based on people that Ormes knew personally&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["98caec36-6c34-40cd-80b3-395002111565"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The text describes how <em>Torchy Brown</em> depicted the experiences of a young Black woman experiencing America&rsquo;s Great Migration. It further states that Ormes continued to use comics throughout her career to humorously comment on important issues impacting Black Americans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text never mentions other Black women comic strip writers. Choice B is incorrect. The text never mentions any changes in the subjects Ormes addressed. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions the inspiration for characters in <em>Torchy Brown</em>. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$41cdb387-651f-4e7b-9f5d-7b3e2b618242$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0ed94d4c","external_id":"fa3b0868-3f29-4a0e-abca-9354255b5f4a","vaultid":"238db56f-cc1d-42d9-80c0-27d1c1e3f154","uId":"acd6b2f3-e160-4d5f-89b8-43ff4ec933f5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jackie Ormes&rsquo;s <em>Torchy Brown in Dixie to Harlem</em> (1937&ndash;38) was the first comic strip by a Black woman to appear in a widely read newspaper. The strip tells the story of Torchy, a young woman who leaves Mississippi to become a performer in New York City. Torchy&rsquo;s story reflects the experience of the Great Migration (1910&ndash;1970), when millions of Black Americans left the South in search of opportunities in other parts of the United States. <em>Torchy Brown</em> thus shows how Ormes used comics to comment humorously on issues affecting Black Americans, which she continued to do throughout her career.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c2f91fa1-8abf-43e3-bec9-d5169d94a871","content":"<p>To show how Ormes&rsquo;s <em>Torchy Brown</em> inspired other Black women to write comic strips in the 1930s</p>"},{"id":"ccae3f88-31a5-49be-abdd-be429b1f9032","content":"<p>To illustrate how the subjects Ormes addressed in her comic strips changed over the course of her career</p>"},{"id":"98caec36-6c34-40cd-80b3-395002111565","content":"<p>To give an example of how Ormes presented the experiences of Black Americans in her comic strips&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e98c3561-4a43-4ab6-a35f-1fddf756c3bf","content":"<p>To claim that several characters in <em>Torchy Brown</em> were based on people that Ormes knew personally&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["98caec36-6c34-40cd-80b3-395002111565"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The text describes how <em>Torchy Brown</em> depicted the experiences of a young Black woman experiencing America&rsquo;s Great Migration. It further states that Ormes continued to use comics throughout her career to humorously comment on important issues impacting Black Americans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text never mentions other Black women comic strip writers. Choice B is incorrect. The text never mentions any changes in the subjects Ormes addressed. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions the inspiration for characters in <em>Torchy Brown</em>. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"41cdb387-651f-4e7b-9f5d-7b3e2b618242","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$0ee67e09$SATQ$, $SATQ$968e7f76-b51a-491c-bdfe-921f5d4e6b46$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7a081498-2a46-421f-a5bc-be102b7c7804$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Anthropologist Kristian J. Carlson and colleagues examined the fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones of a 3.6-million-year-old early hominin known as &ldquo;Little Foot.&rdquo; They found that these bones were <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the clavicle and shoulder bones of modern apes that are frequent climbers, such as gorillas and chimpanzees, suggesting that Little Foot had adapted to life in the trees.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6d10143c-e050-4e2c-96bb-a9a9c15097d0","content":"<p>surpassed by</p>"},{"id":"a30b4875-15a0-45e9-8cf2-2439bd84ffee","content":"<p>comparable to</p>"},{"id":"a73a7eb6-8ed9-42e4-9069-43447d9174a0","content":"<p>independent of</p>"},{"id":"a164df75-222b-41b8-acfc-d74a4dcad81c","content":"<p>obtained from</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a30b4875-15a0-45e9-8cf2-2439bd84ffee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fossilized bones of the hominin known as Little Foot. As used in this context, &ldquo;comparable to&rdquo; would mean similar to. The text indicates that the relationship between the fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones of Little Foot and the clavicle and shoulder bones of &ldquo;frequent climbers,&rdquo; such as chimpanzees and gorillas, suggests that Little Foot had adapted to moving around in trees. This context suggests that the relationship between the fossilized bones of Little Foot and the bones of chimpanzees and gorillas is one of similarity&mdash;the Little Foot fossils are likely comparable to the modern ape bones.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if the fossilized bones of Little Foot were &ldquo;surpassed by,&rdquo; or exceeded by or made inferior to, the bones of modern apes that are frequent climbers, it wouldn&rsquo;t suggest, as the text says, that Little Foot was adapted to moving around in trees. If anything, learning that Little Foot&rsquo;s clavicle and shoulder bones were surpassed by those of chimpanzees and gorillas would suggest that Little Foot was poorly adapted to climbing.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because if Little Foot&rsquo;s fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones were &ldquo;independent of,&rdquo; or not influenced by or affiliated with, the bones of modern apes that climb often, it wouldn&rsquo;t suggest, as the text says, that Little Foot was adapted to moving around in trees.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that Little Foot&rsquo;s fossilized bones date to 3.6 million years ago, so they couldn&rsquo;t have been &ldquo;obtained from,&rdquo; or acquired from, the bones of modern apes.&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-923-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5d87c92c-0017-4b4e-8355-93e2aab3d592$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0ee67e09","external_id":"968e7f76-b51a-491c-bdfe-921f5d4e6b46","vaultid":"7a081498-2a46-421f-a5bc-be102b7c7804","uId":"7d544a37-27da-4c45-ab3c-b5a5bc784051","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Anthropologist Kristian J. Carlson and colleagues examined the fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones of a 3.6-million-year-old early hominin known as &ldquo;Little Foot.&rdquo; They found that these bones were <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the clavicle and shoulder bones of modern apes that are frequent climbers, such as gorillas and chimpanzees, suggesting that Little Foot had adapted to life in the trees.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6d10143c-e050-4e2c-96bb-a9a9c15097d0","content":"<p>surpassed by</p>"},{"id":"a30b4875-15a0-45e9-8cf2-2439bd84ffee","content":"<p>comparable to</p>"},{"id":"a73a7eb6-8ed9-42e4-9069-43447d9174a0","content":"<p>independent of</p>"},{"id":"a164df75-222b-41b8-acfc-d74a4dcad81c","content":"<p>obtained from</p>"}],"keys":["a30b4875-15a0-45e9-8cf2-2439bd84ffee"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the fossilized bones of the hominin known as Little Foot. As used in this context, &ldquo;comparable to&rdquo; would mean similar to. The text indicates that the relationship between the fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones of Little Foot and the clavicle and shoulder bones of &ldquo;frequent climbers,&rdquo; such as chimpanzees and gorillas, suggests that Little Foot had adapted to moving around in trees. This context suggests that the relationship between the fossilized bones of Little Foot and the bones of chimpanzees and gorillas is one of similarity&mdash;the Little Foot fossils are likely comparable to the modern ape bones.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if the fossilized bones of Little Foot were &ldquo;surpassed by,&rdquo; or exceeded by or made inferior to, the bones of modern apes that are frequent climbers, it wouldn&rsquo;t suggest, as the text says, that Little Foot was adapted to moving around in trees. If anything, learning that Little Foot&rsquo;s clavicle and shoulder bones were surpassed by those of chimpanzees and gorillas would suggest that Little Foot was poorly adapted to climbing.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because if Little Foot&rsquo;s fossilized clavicle and shoulder bones were &ldquo;independent of,&rdquo; or not influenced by or affiliated with, the bones of modern apes that climb often, it wouldn&rsquo;t suggest, as the text says, that Little Foot was adapted to moving around in trees.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that Little Foot&rsquo;s fossilized bones date to 3.6 million years ago, so they couldn&rsquo;t have been &ldquo;obtained from,&rdquo; or acquired from, the bones of modern apes.&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-923-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5d87c92c-0017-4b4e-8355-93e2aab3d592","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959355,"updateDate":1691007959355}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959355, 1691007959355),
    ($SATQ$0f040c50$SATQ$, $SATQ$7740dcbf-6582-4063-b1a2-01dbe8e08128$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b90cf24b-4b82-4436-a3d5-bb49b75b19e1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Yann Martel&rsquo;s 2001 novel <em>Life of Pi</em>. The narrator&rsquo;s family owned a zoo when he was a child.&nbsp;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It was a huge zoo, <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>spread</u></span> over numberless acres, big enough to require a train to explore it, though it seemed to get smaller as I grew older, train included.&nbsp;</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;2001 by Yann Martel</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;spread&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a4110fca-339c-4a25-a125-1d17d68f2ba1","content":"<p>Hidden</p>"},{"id":"6c7385a3-3c64-4a11-9bb4-f7ed3ff8f5f2","content":"<p>Discussed</p>"},{"id":"88850f1d-4144-48d7-92c3-b11f5728f784","content":"<p>Extended</p>"},{"id":"05c7edce-0354-4add-a722-060736ecd9ff","content":"<p>Coated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["88850f1d-4144-48d7-92c3-b11f5728f784"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;spread&rdquo; most nearly means extended. The text states that the zoo is &ldquo;huge,&rdquo; that it covers &ldquo;numberless acres,&rdquo; and that it is large enough that a train is needed to explore it. Thus, the text&rsquo;s emphasis on the zoo&rsquo;s size suggests that the zoo extended, or stretched, over a large area of land. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if the zoo covers &ldquo;numberless acres,&rdquo; then it could not reasonably be described as hidden, or concealed from view. Choice B is incorrect because there is nothing in the text to suggest that the zoo was discussed, or talked about. Rather, the text focuses on the zoo&rsquo;s large size.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;spread&rdquo; can mean coated, it doesn&rsquo;t have that meaning in this context because to coat something means to apply a thin layer of a liquid substance, such as oil or paint, to a surface. Therefore, it would not be accurate to say that the zoo coated the acres on which it sits.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-E005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$73eca24b-ec50-4d1f-a3d4-aa5b68e2d5f2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0f040c50","external_id":"7740dcbf-6582-4063-b1a2-01dbe8e08128","vaultid":"b90cf24b-4b82-4436-a3d5-bb49b75b19e1","uId":"1326b254-e81e-4d13-98b6-5ebf58920ff8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Yann Martel&rsquo;s 2001 novel <em>Life of Pi</em>. The narrator&rsquo;s family owned a zoo when he was a child.&nbsp;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It was a huge zoo, <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>spread</u></span> over numberless acres, big enough to require a train to explore it, though it seemed to get smaller as I grew older, train included.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;2001 by Yann Martel</span></p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;spread&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a4110fca-339c-4a25-a125-1d17d68f2ba1","content":"<p>Hidden</p>"},{"id":"6c7385a3-3c64-4a11-9bb4-f7ed3ff8f5f2","content":"<p>Discussed</p>"},{"id":"88850f1d-4144-48d7-92c3-b11f5728f784","content":"<p>Extended</p>"},{"id":"05c7edce-0354-4add-a722-060736ecd9ff","content":"<p>Coated</p>"}],"keys":["88850f1d-4144-48d7-92c3-b11f5728f784"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;spread&rdquo; most nearly means extended. The text states that the zoo is &ldquo;huge,&rdquo; that it covers &ldquo;numberless acres,&rdquo; and that it is large enough that a train is needed to explore it. Thus, the text&rsquo;s emphasis on the zoo&rsquo;s size suggests that the zoo extended, or stretched, over a large area of land. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because if the zoo covers &ldquo;numberless acres,&rdquo; then it could not reasonably be described as hidden, or concealed from view. Choice B is incorrect because there is nothing in the text to suggest that the zoo was discussed, or talked about. Rather, the text focuses on the zoo&rsquo;s large size.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;spread&rdquo; can mean coated, it doesn&rsquo;t have that meaning in this context because to coat something means to apply a thin layer of a liquid substance, such as oil or paint, to a surface. Therefore, it would not be accurate to say that the zoo coated the acres on which it sits.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-E005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"73eca24b-ec50-4d1f-a3d4-aa5b68e2d5f2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$0f2933b9$SATQ$, $SATQ$12555f35-ee6c-44a8-a5e3-33a7ee95ffa6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><p>The following text is from William Shakespeare&rsquo;s circa <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1611</span> play <em>The Winter&rsquo;s Tale</em>. Camillo has been away from his home in Sicily and serves in the court of Polixenes, the king of Bohemia. He has asked Polixenes for permission to return to Sicily.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>POLIXENES: I pray thee, good Camillo, be no more&nbsp;</p>
<p>importunate. &rsquo;Tis a sickness denying thee anything,&nbsp;</p>
<p>a death to grant this.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>CAMILLO: It is fifteen years since I saw my country.</p>
<p>Though I have for the most part been aired abroad,</p>
<p>I desire to lay my bones there. Besides, the penitent</p>
<p>king, my master, hath sent for me, <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><span style="text-decoration: underline;">to whose feeling</span><br/><span style="text-decoration: underline;">sorrows I might be some allay&mdash;or I o&rsquo;erween [presume] to</span><br/><span style="text-decoration: underline;">think so</span></span>&mdash;which is another spur to my departure.</p>
</blockquote></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b2cf4b58-66d7-48f5-af23-733dc5eaf2b0","content":"<p>It establishes Camillo&rsquo;s hope that Polixenes will be comforted knowing that Camillo isn&rsquo;t departing at his own discretion.</p>"},{"id":"cb4e59fa-4cfc-4436-9bed-2c0b3461c410","content":"<p>It suggests that Camillo feels compelled to persuade Polixenes that Camillo&rsquo;s decision to leave the court is justified.</p>"},{"id":"45891951-5071-4015-8df0-a51b44d96ec1","content":"<p>It conveys Camillo&rsquo;s recognition that one benefit his presence may afford the king of Sicily is merely speculative.</p>"},{"id":"f1a86afc-a08d-47ad-9d58-3841fe9f8dc6","content":"<p>It bolsters the idea that Camillo&rsquo;s primary motivation to return home is his concern for the king of Sicily&rsquo;s well-being in his absence.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["45891951-5071-4015-8df0-a51b44d96ec1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. In the text, Camillo explains to Polixenes that he wants to return home after his long absence and that Sicily&rsquo;s king has summoned him. In the underlined portion, Camillo adds that he also wants to return because he might provide comfort to the king&rsquo;s sorrows (&ldquo;to whose feeling sorrows I might be some allay&rdquo;) but then clarifies that his belief about being able to comfort the king is a presumption&mdash;that is, something he thinks is true but can&rsquo;t be certain of. Thus, the underlined portion indicates that Camillo recognizes that one benefit his presence may offer is merely speculative.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined portion conveys Camillo&rsquo;s hope that he might comfort the king of Sicily, which does suggest a personal choice contributing to Camillo&rsquo;s decision to leave; the idea that Camillo isn&rsquo;t choosing to leave is established in a different part of the text, when he says &ldquo;the penitent king, my master, hath sent for me.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t suggest Camillo feels compelled to justify his decision to leave. Rather than expressing a need to persuade Polixenes that his departure is warranted, Camillo&rsquo;s statement simply reveals his awareness that he assumes rather than knows with certainty that his presence will comfort the king of Sicily. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text indicates that Camillo&rsquo;s primary motivation for returning to Sicily is his concern for the king&rsquo;s well-being; Camillo mentions first that he wants to return home because he has been away for so long, then adds that the king has summoned him and that he hopes his presence will comfort the king. The underlined portion simply conveys Camillo&rsquo;s awareness that offering comfort is a hope rather than a certainty; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the king&rsquo;s well-being is his main concern.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0f2933b9","external_id":"12555f35-ee6c-44a8-a5e3-33a7ee95ffa6","vaultid":null,"uId":"5373bfd9-80de-46e0-939a-0e5aa4b779fe","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><p>The following text is from William Shakespeare&rsquo;s circa <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1611</span> play <em>The Winter&rsquo;s Tale</em>. Camillo has been away from his home in Sicily and serves in the court of Polixenes, the king of Bohemia. He has asked Polixenes for permission to return to Sicily.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>POLIXENES: I pray thee, good Camillo, be no more&nbsp;</p>\n<p>importunate. &rsquo;Tis a sickness denying thee anything,&nbsp;</p>\n<p>a death to grant this.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>CAMILLO: It is fifteen years since I saw my country.</p>\n<p>Though I have for the most part been aired abroad,</p>\n<p>I desire to lay my bones there. Besides, the penitent</p>\n<p>king, my master, hath sent for me, <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">to whose feeling</span><br/><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">sorrows I might be some allay&mdash;or I o&rsquo;erween [presume] to</span><br/><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">think so</span></span>&mdash;which is another spur to my departure.</p>\n</blockquote></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b2cf4b58-66d7-48f5-af23-733dc5eaf2b0","content":"<p>It establishes Camillo&rsquo;s hope that Polixenes will be comforted knowing that Camillo isn&rsquo;t departing at his own discretion.</p>"},{"id":"cb4e59fa-4cfc-4436-9bed-2c0b3461c410","content":"<p>It suggests that Camillo feels compelled to persuade Polixenes that Camillo&rsquo;s decision to leave the court is justified.</p>"},{"id":"45891951-5071-4015-8df0-a51b44d96ec1","content":"<p>It conveys Camillo&rsquo;s recognition that one benefit his presence may afford the king of Sicily is merely speculative.</p>"},{"id":"f1a86afc-a08d-47ad-9d58-3841fe9f8dc6","content":"<p>It bolsters the idea that Camillo&rsquo;s primary motivation to return home is his concern for the king of Sicily&rsquo;s well-being in his absence.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["45891951-5071-4015-8df0-a51b44d96ec1"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. In the text, Camillo explains to Polixenes that he wants to return home after his long absence and that Sicily&rsquo;s king has summoned him. In the underlined portion, Camillo adds that he also wants to return because he might provide comfort to the king&rsquo;s sorrows (&ldquo;to whose feeling sorrows I might be some allay&rdquo;) but then clarifies that his belief about being able to comfort the king is a presumption&mdash;that is, something he thinks is true but can&rsquo;t be certain of. Thus, the underlined portion indicates that Camillo recognizes that one benefit his presence may offer is merely speculative.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined portion conveys Camillo&rsquo;s hope that he might comfort the king of Sicily, which does suggest a personal choice contributing to Camillo&rsquo;s decision to leave; the idea that Camillo isn&rsquo;t choosing to leave is established in a different part of the text, when he says &ldquo;the penitent king, my master, hath sent for me.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t suggest Camillo feels compelled to justify his decision to leave. Rather than expressing a need to persuade Polixenes that his departure is warranted, Camillo&rsquo;s statement simply reveals his awareness that he assumes rather than knows with certainty that his presence will comfort the king of Sicily. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text indicates that Camillo&rsquo;s primary motivation for returning to Sicily is his concern for the king&rsquo;s well-being; Camillo mentions first that he wants to return home because he has been away for so long, then adds that the king has summoned him and that he hopes his presence will comfort the king. The underlined portion simply conveys Camillo&rsquo;s awareness that offering comfort is a hope rather than a certainty; it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the king&rsquo;s well-being is his main concern.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771167,"updateDate":1776362771167}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771167, 1776362771167),
    ($SATQ$105ea6de$SATQ$, $SATQ$c088488a-21d9-4bb9-b70e-a72699d804ca$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$dfb969ab-e004-4d7d-be32-ef931299127a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Text 1</p>
<p>Growth in the use of novel nanohybrids&mdash;materials created from the conjugation of multiple distinct nanomaterials, such as iron oxide and gold nanomaterials conjugated for use in magnetic imaging&mdash;has outpaced studies of nanohybrids&rsquo; environmental risks. Unfortunately, risk evaluations based on nanohybrids&rsquo; constituents are not reliable: <u>conjugation may alter constituents&rsquo; physiochemical properties such that innocuous nanomaterials form a nanohybrid that is anything but</u>.</p>
<p>Text 2</p>
<p>The potential for enhanced toxicity of nanohybrids relative to the toxicity of constituent nanomaterials has drawn deserved attention, but the effects of nanomaterial conjugation vary by case. For instance, it was recently shown that a nanohybrid of silicon dioxide and zinc oxide preserved the desired optical transparency of zinc oxide nanoparticles while mitigating the nanoparticles&rsquo; potential to damage DNA.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the assertion in the underlined portion of Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"885fd13f-9a55-4a12-97e3-05f1bc79f67c","content":"<p>By concurring that the risk described in Text 1 should be evaluated but emphasizing that the risk is more than offset by the potential benefits of nanomaterial conjugation</p>"},{"id":"4cd7f2a4-356a-4e4f-a9ff-90fc07001ad0","content":"<p>By arguing that the situation described in Text 1 may not be representative but conceding that the effects of nanomaterial conjugation are harder to predict than researchers had expected</p>"},{"id":"98c8c4a4-9ebd-4b52-9f62-dfd4daed3f9f","content":"<p>By denying that the circumstance described in Text 1 is likely to occur but acknowledging that many aspects of nanomaterial conjugation are still poorly understood</p>"},{"id":"9a7aec19-0979-4be1-9c70-973e29f6518b","content":"<p>By agreeing that the possibility described in Text 1 is a cause for concern but pointing out that nanomaterial conjugation does not inevitably produce that result</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9a7aec19-0979-4be1-9c70-973e29f6518b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The author of Text 2 acknowledges that nanohybrids may be more toxic than their constituent parts, but also provides an example of a nanohybrid that has reduced toxicity compared to its components: silicon dioxide and zinc oxide together have all the benefits of zinc oxide nanoparticles without any of the DNA harm zinc oxide has on its own. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the author of Text 2 gives an example of a nanohybrid that isn&rsquo;t as toxic as its constituent parts, they don&rsquo;t argue that the benefit outweighs the risk. They merely argue that &ldquo;the effects of nanomaterial conjugation vary by case.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. The author of Text 2 states that the effects of nanomaterial conjugation &ldquo;vary by case,&rdquo; and that the attention that their potential toxicity has drawn is warranted. If the situation in Text 1 weren&rsquo;t representative, then there would be less attention to the potential danger of these materials. Furthermore, neither passage suggests that researchers had expected that they could predict the effects of nanomaterial conjugation. Choice C is incorrect. The author of Text 2 agrees that the potential toxicity of nanohybrids &ldquo;has drawn deserved attention,&rdquo; so they aren&rsquo;t denying the problem. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e2077839-08b7-4da6-9363-d7647db8e7af$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"105ea6de","external_id":"c088488a-21d9-4bb9-b70e-a72699d804ca","vaultid":"dfb969ab-e004-4d7d-be32-ef931299127a","uId":"19df0edd-94fd-4dd1-8c2f-87f4eca5a09f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Text 1</p>\n<p>Growth in the use of novel nanohybrids&mdash;materials created from the conjugation of multiple distinct nanomaterials, such as iron oxide and gold nanomaterials conjugated for use in magnetic imaging&mdash;has outpaced studies of nanohybrids&rsquo; environmental risks. Unfortunately, risk evaluations based on nanohybrids&rsquo; constituents are not reliable: <u>conjugation may alter constituents&rsquo; physiochemical properties such that innocuous nanomaterials form a nanohybrid that is anything but</u>.</p>\n<p>Text 2</p>\n<p>The potential for enhanced toxicity of nanohybrids relative to the toxicity of constituent nanomaterials has drawn deserved attention, but the effects of nanomaterial conjugation vary by case. For instance, it was recently shown that a nanohybrid of silicon dioxide and zinc oxide preserved the desired optical transparency of zinc oxide nanoparticles while mitigating the nanoparticles&rsquo; potential to damage DNA.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the assertion in the underlined portion of Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"885fd13f-9a55-4a12-97e3-05f1bc79f67c","content":"<p>By concurring that the risk described in Text 1 should be evaluated but emphasizing that the risk is more than offset by the potential benefits of nanomaterial conjugation</p>"},{"id":"4cd7f2a4-356a-4e4f-a9ff-90fc07001ad0","content":"<p>By arguing that the situation described in Text 1 may not be representative but conceding that the effects of nanomaterial conjugation are harder to predict than researchers had expected</p>"},{"id":"98c8c4a4-9ebd-4b52-9f62-dfd4daed3f9f","content":"<p>By denying that the circumstance described in Text 1 is likely to occur but acknowledging that many aspects of nanomaterial conjugation are still poorly understood</p>"},{"id":"9a7aec19-0979-4be1-9c70-973e29f6518b","content":"<p>By agreeing that the possibility described in Text 1 is a cause for concern but pointing out that nanomaterial conjugation does not inevitably produce that result</p>"}],"keys":["9a7aec19-0979-4be1-9c70-973e29f6518b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The author of Text 2 acknowledges that nanohybrids may be more toxic than their constituent parts, but also provides an example of a nanohybrid that has reduced toxicity compared to its components: silicon dioxide and zinc oxide together have all the benefits of zinc oxide nanoparticles without any of the DNA harm zinc oxide has on its own. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the author of Text 2 gives an example of a nanohybrid that isn&rsquo;t as toxic as its constituent parts, they don&rsquo;t argue that the benefit outweighs the risk. They merely argue that &ldquo;the effects of nanomaterial conjugation vary by case.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. The author of Text 2 states that the effects of nanomaterial conjugation &ldquo;vary by case,&rdquo; and that the attention that their potential toxicity has drawn is warranted. If the situation in Text 1 weren&rsquo;t representative, then there would be less attention to the potential danger of these materials. Furthermore, neither passage suggests that researchers had expected that they could predict the effects of nanomaterial conjugation. Choice C is incorrect. The author of Text 2 agrees that the potential toxicity of nanohybrids &ldquo;has drawn deserved attention,&rdquo; so they aren&rsquo;t denying the problem. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e2077839-08b7-4da6-9363-d7647db8e7af","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$1090b367$SATQ$, $SATQ$086ba4d2-8f92-425d-ae4f-c95cecbd3a60$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Today composer Scott Joplin is mainly celebrated for his catchy ragtime pieces &ldquo;Maple Leaf Rag&rdquo; and &ldquo;The Entertainer.&rdquo; However, by overlooking his less famous works, listeners will miss the full range of Joplin&rsquo;s creativity. For instance, his waltz &ldquo;Pleasant Moments&rdquo; and his opera <em>Treemonisha</em> skillfully blend ragtime and classical music. These masterpieces deserve as much fame as Joplin&rsquo;s biggest hits.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"304fdf40-3905-47c3-8270-87d4b8afaf9d","content":"<p>To describe the similarities and differences between ragtime music and opera</p>"},{"id":"36e238df-45a5-4869-99ef-195f0fcafb3c","content":"<p>To argue that more attention should be given to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works</p>"},{"id":"72eb399f-14f5-43ca-ad29-020c7afce111","content":"<p>To encourage music lovers to listen to music by many different composers</p>"},{"id":"9f780935-47ba-4647-882c-f4e7eab98557","content":"<p>To explain how Joplin learned to compose and perform ragtime music</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["36e238df-45a5-4869-99ef-195f0fcafb3c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best states the main purpose of the text, which is to make a case for the importance of Scott Joplin&rsquo;s less famous works. The text begins by introducing Joplin&rsquo;s most popular works and then goes on to assert that gaining a full understanding of Joplin&rsquo;s creativity requires a consideration of his lesser-known pieces as well. The text concludes by characterizing the lesser-known works "Pleasant Moments" and <em>Treemonisha</em> as masterpieces that deserve to be famous. These details indicate that the text&rsquo;s main purpose is to argue that more attention should be given to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses some of the ragtime music and an opera that Joplin composed, it does not compare the two types of music in general. Instead, the text argues that Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works, including his opera, deserve as much attention as his more famous ragtime pieces receive. Choice C is incorrect because the text discusses only Joplin&rsquo;s music and does not ask music lovers to listen to a variety of composers. Instead, the text encourages listeners to pay attention to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works in order to gain a full understanding of his creativity. Choice D is incorrect because the text does not discuss how Joplin learned to compose and perform ragtime music. Instead, the text focuses on Joplin&rsquo;s less famous works and makes a case for their importance.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1090b367","external_id":"086ba4d2-8f92-425d-ae4f-c95cecbd3a60","vaultid":null,"uId":"fbe51458-49ab-47e9-81c2-97eef07bd4f3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Today composer Scott Joplin is mainly celebrated for his catchy ragtime pieces &ldquo;Maple Leaf Rag&rdquo; and &ldquo;The Entertainer.&rdquo; However, by overlooking his less famous works, listeners will miss the full range of Joplin&rsquo;s creativity. For instance, his waltz &ldquo;Pleasant Moments&rdquo; and his opera <em>Treemonisha</em> skillfully blend ragtime and classical music. These masterpieces deserve as much fame as Joplin&rsquo;s biggest hits.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"304fdf40-3905-47c3-8270-87d4b8afaf9d","content":"<p>To describe the similarities and differences between ragtime music and opera</p>"},{"id":"36e238df-45a5-4869-99ef-195f0fcafb3c","content":"<p>To argue that more attention should be given to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works</p>"},{"id":"72eb399f-14f5-43ca-ad29-020c7afce111","content":"<p>To encourage music lovers to listen to music by many different composers</p>"},{"id":"9f780935-47ba-4647-882c-f4e7eab98557","content":"<p>To explain how Joplin learned to compose and perform ragtime music</p>"}],"keys":["36e238df-45a5-4869-99ef-195f0fcafb3c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best states the main purpose of the text, which is to make a case for the importance of Scott Joplin&rsquo;s less famous works. The text begins by introducing Joplin&rsquo;s most popular works and then goes on to assert that gaining a full understanding of Joplin&rsquo;s creativity requires a consideration of his lesser-known pieces as well. The text concludes by characterizing the lesser-known works \"Pleasant Moments\" and <em>Treemonisha</em> as masterpieces that deserve to be famous. These details indicate that the text&rsquo;s main purpose is to argue that more attention should be given to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses some of the ragtime music and an opera that Joplin composed, it does not compare the two types of music in general. Instead, the text argues that Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works, including his opera, deserve as much attention as his more famous ragtime pieces receive. Choice C is incorrect because the text discusses only Joplin&rsquo;s music and does not ask music lovers to listen to a variety of composers. Instead, the text encourages listeners to pay attention to Joplin&rsquo;s lesser-known works in order to gain a full understanding of his creativity. Choice D is incorrect because the text does not discuss how Joplin learned to compose and perform ragtime music. Instead, the text focuses on Joplin&rsquo;s less famous works and makes a case for their importance.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323040,"updateDate":1730147323040}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323040, 1730147323040),
    ($SATQ$10a409bd$SATQ$, $SATQ$80854706-ff1b-4973-95ea-631207d93c52$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Ida <span data-ssml-sub-alias="B">B.</span> Wells&rsquo;s 1970 autobiography <em>A Crusade for Justice.</em> Mr. Watts is a reference to George Frederic Watts, an English painter.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[Manchester&rsquo;s] art galleries are so&nbsp;<span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">arranged</span>&nbsp;that the name of every picture is plainly seen and one has no need of a catalogue to pick out the name and the artist. This is a convenience to the general public, which other art galleries, which shall be nameless, might copy to advantage. To her treasure of art Manchester has added Mr. Watts&rsquo; latest picture, the Good Samaritan.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;1970 by the University of Chicago Press</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;arranged&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"11bcb8cf-30af-4ba5-aeb5-45522332ee48","content":"<p>Organized</p>"},{"id":"68e0743a-b703-473e-8254-5a39e61f8924","content":"<p>Ranked</p>"},{"id":"7fb3729a-f0c1-4b52-a2ba-fa887fbae3e3","content":"<p>Scheduled</p>"},{"id":"ebf9becf-9c11-49d8-9dc5-81f7dc6607f6","content":"<p>Discussed</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["11bcb8cf-30af-4ba5-aeb5-45522332ee48"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "arranged" most nearly means "organized." The text states that a welcome feature of Manchester&rsquo;s art galleries (as opposed to others) is that the titles of the paintings are easy to see and that a catalogue is therefore unneeded while visitors browse the art galleries. This suggests that the paintings are displayed in a systematic, or organized, way.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. "Ranked" might mean either classified or placed in orderly rows. Neither of these meanings would necessarily imply that the names of the paintings are easy to see (even if the paintings are placed in rows, their labels might be obscured). Choice C is incorrect because it would not make sense in context to describe the paintings as "scheduled," or planned to take place at a certain time. Choice D is incorrect because if the paintings were "discussed," or talked about, this would have no bearing on whether the names of the paintings could be easily seen.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"10a409bd","external_id":"80854706-ff1b-4973-95ea-631207d93c52","vaultid":null,"uId":"99b5a006-f2c9-44e2-b337-8e9a49bd2fc3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Ida <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"B\">B.</span> Wells&rsquo;s 1970 autobiography <em>A Crusade for Justice.</em> Mr. Watts is a reference to George Frederic Watts, an English painter.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[Manchester&rsquo;s] art galleries are so&nbsp;<span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">arranged</span>&nbsp;that the name of every picture is plainly seen and one has no need of a catalogue to pick out the name and the artist. This is a convenience to the general public, which other art galleries, which shall be nameless, might copy to advantage. To her treasure of art Manchester has added Mr. Watts&rsquo; latest picture, the Good Samaritan.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;1970 by the University of Chicago Press</span></p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;arranged&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"11bcb8cf-30af-4ba5-aeb5-45522332ee48","content":"<p>Organized</p>"},{"id":"68e0743a-b703-473e-8254-5a39e61f8924","content":"<p>Ranked</p>"},{"id":"7fb3729a-f0c1-4b52-a2ba-fa887fbae3e3","content":"<p>Scheduled</p>"},{"id":"ebf9becf-9c11-49d8-9dc5-81f7dc6607f6","content":"<p>Discussed</p>"}],"keys":["11bcb8cf-30af-4ba5-aeb5-45522332ee48"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"arranged\" most nearly means \"organized.\" The text states that a welcome feature of Manchester&rsquo;s art galleries (as opposed to others) is that the titles of the paintings are easy to see and that a catalogue is therefore unneeded while visitors browse the art galleries. This suggests that the paintings are displayed in a systematic, or organized, way.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. \"Ranked\" might mean either classified or placed in orderly rows. Neither of these meanings would necessarily imply that the names of the paintings are easy to see (even if the paintings are placed in rows, their labels might be obscured). Choice C is incorrect because it would not make sense in context to describe the paintings as \"scheduled,\" or planned to take place at a certain time. Choice D is incorrect because if the paintings were \"discussed,\" or talked about, this would have no bearing on whether the names of the paintings could be easily seen.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669210,"updateDate":1743430669210}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669210, 1743430669210),
    ($SATQ$10c236ce$SATQ$, $SATQ$66ed1573-e520-4752-905b-e67ad85b68a4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Text corpora such as the Bank of English are enormous collections of electronically stored texts that can be used for empirical testing of hypotheses regarding the frequency of typical word usage. If one has a <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> that the word &ldquo;get&rdquo; has a high incidence in English, for example, an analysis of a corpus can support that hypothesis by showing that &ldquo;get&rdquo; is the fifth most commonly used verb.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ff25669d-1de8-4fc7-a11f-d5eff97cebc8","content":"<p>recognition</p>"},{"id":"d1165374-ae9e-4987-8712-ec579804409b","content":"<p>scheme</p>"},{"id":"547b6bec-1484-479c-8f7e-1bb458b0b103","content":"<p>supposition</p>"},{"id":"74eff1cd-cc86-4b69-a76b-4568644bbc61","content":"<p>synopsis</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["547b6bec-1484-479c-8f7e-1bb458b0b103"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of testing hypotheses regarding usage by means of text corpora. In this context, "supposition" refers to an assumption or hypothesis that is not yet proven. The text explains that text corpora can be used for "empirical testing of hypotheses regarding the frequency of typical word usage" and then provides a specific example of using corpus analysis to support a claim about the word "get." The word "supposition" precisely conveys this idea of an assumption or hypothesis about word frequency that can then be tested against empirical data.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a "recognition" that the word "get" has a high incidence in English. Recognition implies acknowledging something already established as true or valid, but the text indicates that corpus analysis is needed to support the claim about "get," suggesting its high frequency wasn&rsquo;t already established or previously known. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a "scheme" that the word "get" has a high incidence in English. A scheme is a plan or systematic arrangement, but the context indicates what&rsquo;s needed is a preliminary hypothesis that can be tested with corpus data, not a plan related to word frequency. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a "synopsis" that the word "get" has a high incidence in English. A synopsis is a summary or outline, but the text is discussing an initial hypothesis about word frequency that requires testing, not a condensed statement of existing information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"10c236ce","external_id":"66ed1573-e520-4752-905b-e67ad85b68a4","vaultid":null,"uId":"5e2b94f2-14f4-4aa2-b02a-6aaa64a8c7a2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Text corpora such as the Bank of English are enormous collections of electronically stored texts that can be used for empirical testing of hypotheses regarding the frequency of typical word usage. If one has a <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> that the word &ldquo;get&rdquo; has a high incidence in English, for example, an analysis of a corpus can support that hypothesis by showing that &ldquo;get&rdquo; is the fifth most commonly used verb.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ff25669d-1de8-4fc7-a11f-d5eff97cebc8","content":"<p>recognition</p>"},{"id":"d1165374-ae9e-4987-8712-ec579804409b","content":"<p>scheme</p>"},{"id":"547b6bec-1484-479c-8f7e-1bb458b0b103","content":"<p>supposition</p>"},{"id":"74eff1cd-cc86-4b69-a76b-4568644bbc61","content":"<p>synopsis</p>"}],"keys":["547b6bec-1484-479c-8f7e-1bb458b0b103"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of testing hypotheses regarding usage by means of text corpora. In this context, \"supposition\" refers to an assumption or hypothesis that is not yet proven. The text explains that text corpora can be used for \"empirical testing of hypotheses regarding the frequency of typical word usage\" and then provides a specific example of using corpus analysis to support a claim about the word \"get.\" The word \"supposition\" precisely conveys this idea of an assumption or hypothesis about word frequency that can then be tested against empirical data.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a \"recognition\" that the word \"get\" has a high incidence in English. Recognition implies acknowledging something already established as true or valid, but the text indicates that corpus analysis is needed to support the claim about \"get,\" suggesting its high frequency wasn&rsquo;t already established or previously known. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a \"scheme\" that the word \"get\" has a high incidence in English. A scheme is a plan or systematic arrangement, but the context indicates what&rsquo;s needed is a preliminary hypothesis that can be tested with corpus data, not a plan related to word frequency. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that someone has a \"synopsis\" that the word \"get\" has a high incidence in English. A synopsis is a summary or outline, but the text is discussing an initial hypothesis about word frequency that requires testing, not a condensed statement of existing information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061178,"updateDate":1755117061178}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061178, 1755117061178),
    ($SATQ$1107e7dc$SATQ$, $SATQ$b4582e11-c0ed-4c17-a092-446a9806c811$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Economists often assert that countries looking to increase their reliance on solar energy should expand their capacity for storage; having an ample reserve of stored energy can mitigate the effects of <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> solar energy collection caused by unpredictable shifts in cloud cover and haze.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"15e0596e-b0cc-4986-8b18-2dec1528b9ff","content":"<p>developments of</p>"},{"id":"84688071-6efa-4d8a-9c22-c3132014f246","content":"<p><strong> </strong>fluctuations in</p>"},{"id":"7b5286c0-5043-48de-ab40-2b63df842948","content":"<p>calibrations with</p>"},{"id":"98e31b5e-baff-4c83-a9b9-d510bc6bc297","content":"<p>incentives for</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["84688071-6efa-4d8a-9c22-c3132014f246"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of solar energy collection and storage. In this context, "fluctuations in" means irregular changes in. The text notes that economists recommend that countries that want to rely more on solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) should attempt to expand their storage capacity. The text goes on to explain that having a large amount of stored solar energy can lessen the negative effect of "unpredictable shifts in cloud cover and haze"&mdash;that is, unpredictable changes in environmental conditions that at times limit sunlight. This context suggests that having ample solar energy stored and ready to use can mitigate the issue of fluctuations in solar energy collection caused by irregular patterns of sunlight availability.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "developments of solar energy collection" would most clearly refer to the creation of ways to collect solar energy (energy captured from sunlight). The text focuses on collection and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight ("cloud cover and haze"); there&rsquo;s no reason to think that the creation of new collection methods would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "calibrations with" would most clearly refer to precise adjustments for a particular purpose. The text focuses on the collection of solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight ("cloud cover and haze"); there&rsquo;s no reason to think that precision in something related to solar energy collection would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated. Choice D is incorrect. The text focuses on the collection of solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight ("cloud cover and haze"); nothing in the text suggests that "incentives for," or rewards for, solar energy collection would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated, especially in countries that want to rely more on solar energy than they already do.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1107e7dc","external_id":"b4582e11-c0ed-4c17-a092-446a9806c811","vaultid":null,"uId":"cdfadd49-2e41-47ba-aaa7-0149566b4e83","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Economists often assert that countries looking to increase their reliance on solar energy should expand their capacity for storage; having an ample reserve of stored energy can mitigate the effects of <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> solar energy collection caused by unpredictable shifts in cloud cover and haze.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"15e0596e-b0cc-4986-8b18-2dec1528b9ff","content":"<p>developments of</p>"},{"id":"84688071-6efa-4d8a-9c22-c3132014f246","content":"<p><strong> </strong>fluctuations in</p>"},{"id":"7b5286c0-5043-48de-ab40-2b63df842948","content":"<p>calibrations with</p>"},{"id":"98e31b5e-baff-4c83-a9b9-d510bc6bc297","content":"<p>incentives for</p>"}],"keys":["84688071-6efa-4d8a-9c22-c3132014f246"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of solar energy collection and storage. In this context, \"fluctuations in\" means irregular changes in. The text notes that economists recommend that countries that want to rely more on solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) should attempt to expand their storage capacity. The text goes on to explain that having a large amount of stored solar energy can lessen the negative effect of \"unpredictable shifts in cloud cover and haze\"&mdash;that is, unpredictable changes in environmental conditions that at times limit sunlight. This context suggests that having ample solar energy stored and ready to use can mitigate the issue of fluctuations in solar energy collection caused by irregular patterns of sunlight availability.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"developments of solar energy collection\" would most clearly refer to the creation of ways to collect solar energy (energy captured from sunlight). The text focuses on collection and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight (\"cloud cover and haze\"); there&rsquo;s no reason to think that the creation of new collection methods would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"calibrations with\" would most clearly refer to precise adjustments for a particular purpose. The text focuses on the collection of solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight (\"cloud cover and haze\"); there&rsquo;s no reason to think that precision in something related to solar energy collection would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated. Choice D is incorrect. The text focuses on the collection of solar energy (energy captured from sunlight) and the idea that having stored energy can mitigate, or make less bad, the effects of a problem caused by unpredictable changes that affect sunlight (\"cloud cover and haze\"); nothing in the text suggests that \"incentives for,\" or rewards for, solar energy collection would be viewed as a problem that would need to be mitigated, especially in countries that want to rely more on solar energy than they already do.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$12d81fc1$SATQ$, $SATQ$f80d1e61-9573-4a9f-b2f8-f179a789a2f4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6eb4e2c4-e310-4fab-b01a-ea71e693fc82$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Because literacy in Nahuatl script, the writing system of the Aztec Empire, was lost after Spain invaded central Mexico in the 1500s, it is unclear exactly how meaning was encoded in the script&rsquo;s symbols. Although many scholars had assumed that the symbols signified entire words, linguist Alfonso Lacadena theorized in 2008 that they signified units of language smaller than words: individual syllables.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>The growing consensus among scholars of Nahuatl script is that many of its symbols could signify either words or syllables, depending on syntax and content at any given site within a text. For example, the symbol signifying the word <span lang="nah"><em>huipil</em></span> (blouse) in some contexts could signify the syllable &ldquo;pil&rdquo; in others, as in the place name &ldquo;Chipiltepec.&rdquo; Thus, for the Aztecs, reading required a determination of how such symbols functioned each time they appeared in a text.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize Lacadena&rsquo;s theory, as described in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a8de2eaa-790a-4a29-a2c6-311b076659c3","content":"<p>By praising the theory for recognizing that the script&rsquo;s symbols could represent entire words</p>"},{"id":"031ae1f6-647f-451e-92ad-757947f42368","content":"<p>By arguing that the theory is overly influenced by the work of earlier scholars&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5c188f29-6101-4896-b8f1-b54e2f1922be","content":"<p>By approving of the theory&rsquo;s emphasis on how the script changed over time</p>"},{"id":"39a7e380-4567-44f7-8265-b79d8f53e059","content":"<p>By cautioning that the theory overlooks certain important aspects of how the script functioned</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["39a7e380-4567-44f7-8265-b79d8f53e059"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. Lacadena&rsquo;s theory is that Nahuatl script symbols signified syllables, but the consensus described in Text 2 is that they can signify either symbols or full words, depending on the context. So the author of Text 2 would likely consider Lacadena&rsquo;s theory too simplistic: it&rsquo;s missing the importance of the context in determining the meaning of a symbol. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This conflicts with Text 1&rsquo;s description of Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Lacadena&rsquo;s theory is that Nahuatl script symbols signified syllables. Choice B is incorrect. This conflicts with Text 1&rsquo;s description of Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Text 1 states that Lacadena&rsquo;s theory differed from what earlier scholars believed. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 2 would characterize Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Neither text mentions how or even if the script changed over time. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-M003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1deb9013-a6be-49e4-b299-31a0ee0d451e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"12d81fc1","external_id":"f80d1e61-9573-4a9f-b2f8-f179a789a2f4","vaultid":"6eb4e2c4-e310-4fab-b01a-ea71e693fc82","uId":"a57cac9b-c15c-4aa3-a077-c3e5c94f4f29","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Because literacy in Nahuatl script, the writing system of the Aztec Empire, was lost after Spain invaded central Mexico in the 1500s, it is unclear exactly how meaning was encoded in the script&rsquo;s symbols. Although many scholars had assumed that the symbols signified entire words, linguist Alfonso Lacadena theorized in 2008 that they signified units of language smaller than words: individual syllables.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>The growing consensus among scholars of Nahuatl script is that many of its symbols could signify either words or syllables, depending on syntax and content at any given site within a text. For example, the symbol signifying the word <span lang=\"nah\"><em>huipil</em></span> (blouse) in some contexts could signify the syllable &ldquo;pil&rdquo; in others, as in the place name &ldquo;Chipiltepec.&rdquo; Thus, for the Aztecs, reading required a determination of how such symbols functioned each time they appeared in a text.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize Lacadena&rsquo;s theory, as described in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a8de2eaa-790a-4a29-a2c6-311b076659c3","content":"<p>By praising the theory for recognizing that the script&rsquo;s symbols could represent entire words</p>"},{"id":"031ae1f6-647f-451e-92ad-757947f42368","content":"<p>By arguing that the theory is overly influenced by the work of earlier scholars&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"5c188f29-6101-4896-b8f1-b54e2f1922be","content":"<p>By approving of the theory&rsquo;s emphasis on how the script changed over time</p>"},{"id":"39a7e380-4567-44f7-8265-b79d8f53e059","content":"<p>By cautioning that the theory overlooks certain important aspects of how the script functioned</p>"}],"keys":["39a7e380-4567-44f7-8265-b79d8f53e059"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. Lacadena&rsquo;s theory is that Nahuatl script symbols signified syllables, but the consensus described in Text 2 is that they can signify either symbols or full words, depending on the context. So the author of Text 2 would likely consider Lacadena&rsquo;s theory too simplistic: it&rsquo;s missing the importance of the context in determining the meaning of a symbol. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This conflicts with Text 1&rsquo;s description of Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Lacadena&rsquo;s theory is that Nahuatl script symbols signified syllables. Choice B is incorrect. This conflicts with Text 1&rsquo;s description of Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Text 1 states that Lacadena&rsquo;s theory differed from what earlier scholars believed. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 2 would characterize Lacadena&rsquo;s theory. Neither text mentions how or even if the script changed over time. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-M003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1deb9013-a6be-49e4-b299-31a0ee0d451e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366),
    ($SATQ$1374a9fa$SATQ$, $SATQ$9f230c32-c66e-4279-9476-6ae4fa8d63fe$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Austin McCoy has argued that historians have tended to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> rank-and-file workers in analyses of changes in organized labor in the United States in the decades following the Second World War, presenting political, business, and labor leaders as the major actors and ordinary workers as nearly devoid of agency.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"72075927-f6de-4fe4-9235-ba28806c414c","content":"<p>epitomize</p>"},{"id":"fad90b10-0e42-49cf-b48d-c937589a7e80","content":"<p>marginalize</p>"},{"id":"ced29747-f974-458e-a955-bf0a86c58ce3","content":"<p>extol</p>"},{"id":"33c591f0-9708-4049-b76e-4a52dfe2e063","content":"<p>substantiate</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fad90b10-0e42-49cf-b48d-c937589a7e80"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how historians have treated rank-and-file (ordinary) workers in their analyses. In this context, "marginalize" means treat as unimportant or relatively powerless within a group or society. The text explains that according to Austin McCoy, historians have tended to treat "political, business, and labor leaders" as the "major actors" involved in making changes in organized labor in the period after the Second World War. By emphasizing the contributions of these leaders, historians have in effect minimized the contributions of ordinary workers, treating them as relatively unimportant and "nearly devoid of agency," thereby marginalizing them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, "epitomize" would mean either serve as an ideal example of something or make into an example of something, neither of which would make sense. In stating that historians have tended to characterize political, business, and labor leaders as the primary actors responsible for changes in the post-Second World War labor movement in a manner that minimizes the influence of ordinary workers, the text suggests that historians have tended to present ordinary workers as unimportant to or uninfluential within the movement, not that historians themselves serve as examples of ordinary workers, or that historians in their analyses have made ordinary workers into examples of the changes that occurred. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "extol" would mean praise highly or glorify. The text states that historians have tended to present ordinary workers as being "nearly devoid of agency," thereby minimizing their importance. It therefore wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that in doing so, historians have tended to praise or glorify them. Choice D is incorrect because "substantiate" would mean verify with evidence, and nothing in the text suggests that historians have verified rank-and-file workers or their contributions in their analyses. If anything, the text suggests the opposite, implying that by minimizing workers&rsquo; agency in organized labor, historians have likely overlooked evidence demonstrating their influence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1374a9fa","external_id":"9f230c32-c66e-4279-9476-6ae4fa8d63fe","vaultid":null,"uId":"babb84dd-4fc3-46cc-b12c-1eaa002f15a9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Austin McCoy has argued that historians have tended to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> rank-and-file workers in analyses of changes in organized labor in the United States in the decades following the Second World War, presenting political, business, and labor leaders as the major actors and ordinary workers as nearly devoid of agency.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"72075927-f6de-4fe4-9235-ba28806c414c","content":"<p>epitomize</p>"},{"id":"fad90b10-0e42-49cf-b48d-c937589a7e80","content":"<p>marginalize</p>"},{"id":"ced29747-f974-458e-a955-bf0a86c58ce3","content":"<p>extol</p>"},{"id":"33c591f0-9708-4049-b76e-4a52dfe2e063","content":"<p>substantiate</p>"}],"keys":["fad90b10-0e42-49cf-b48d-c937589a7e80"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how historians have treated rank-and-file (ordinary) workers in their analyses. In this context, \"marginalize\" means treat as unimportant or relatively powerless within a group or society. The text explains that according to Austin McCoy, historians have tended to treat \"political, business, and labor leaders\" as the \"major actors\" involved in making changes in organized labor in the period after the Second World War. By emphasizing the contributions of these leaders, historians have in effect minimized the contributions of ordinary workers, treating them as relatively unimportant and \"nearly devoid of agency,\" thereby marginalizing them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, \"epitomize\" would mean either serve as an ideal example of something or make into an example of something, neither of which would make sense. In stating that historians have tended to characterize political, business, and labor leaders as the primary actors responsible for changes in the post-Second World War labor movement in a manner that minimizes the influence of ordinary workers, the text suggests that historians have tended to present ordinary workers as unimportant to or uninfluential within the movement, not that historians themselves serve as examples of ordinary workers, or that historians in their analyses have made ordinary workers into examples of the changes that occurred. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"extol\" would mean praise highly or glorify. The text states that historians have tended to present ordinary workers as being \"nearly devoid of agency,\" thereby minimizing their importance. It therefore wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that in doing so, historians have tended to praise or glorify them. Choice D is incorrect because \"substantiate\" would mean verify with evidence, and nothing in the text suggests that historians have verified rank-and-file workers or their contributions in their analyses. If anything, the text suggests the opposite, implying that by minimizing workers&rsquo; agency in organized labor, historians have likely overlooked evidence demonstrating their influence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061190,"updateDate":1755117061190}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061190, 1755117061190),
    ($SATQ$1455b754$SATQ$, $SATQ$56a6e247-3219-43b9-8ec1-3e5b2c20ad60$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While recent scholarship has undermined claims that the works of twelfth-century Islamic philosopher Ibn Rushd were <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> other Muslim philosophers of his time, it is indisputable that his location in the Muslim-ruled area of what is now Spain meant that his works were primarily available thousands of miles west of the era&rsquo;s center of Islamic thought.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e85b0a0d-f31d-4e97-a809-56b35ebee3e9","content":"<p>controversial among</p>"},{"id":"3f2e8216-b231-41e6-a6a1-1501af42af48","content":"<p>antagonistic toward</p>"},{"id":"c848db49-6ac0-4f62-b04e-065d6aa16202","content":"<p>imitated by</p>"},{"id":"1d5f1a3d-84f1-4669-a345-0c4f7e8b4a72","content":"<p>inconsequential to</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1d5f1a3d-84f1-4669-a345-0c4f7e8b4a72"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the twelfth-century Islamic philosopher Ibn Rushd. As used in this context, "inconsequential to" means not significant to. According to the text, Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works were primarily available in Spain, where he lived, far from central areas of the Muslim world, a fact that could support the conclusion that his influence on Muslim contemporaries was limited. The text implies, however, that recent scholarship has shown that his works still had an impact on other Muslim philosophers of his time. This context supports the idea that his works weren&rsquo;t inconsequential to Islamic thought in this period.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the issue under consideration in the text is whether other Muslim philosophers of Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s time had access to his works, not whether his works were "controversial among," or causing dispute among, other philosophers. Choice B is incorrect. The text implies that other Muslim philosophers of Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s era were aware of his works, not that they were "antagonistic toward," or hostile toward, them. There is no suggestion that Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s writings elicited hostility from his contemporaries. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "imitated by" would mean followed as a model by. Although the text implies that Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works were at least somewhat available in regions that were at the center of Islamic thought during the period, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically address in what ways his works influenced contemporary Muslim philosophers. Thus, the text doesn&rsquo;t support the idea that other philosophers modeled their own works after Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1455b754","external_id":"56a6e247-3219-43b9-8ec1-3e5b2c20ad60","vaultid":null,"uId":"5cf1842f-b69d-4150-b162-0b02d7c5b5f0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While recent scholarship has undermined claims that the works of twelfth-century Islamic philosopher Ibn Rushd were <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> other Muslim philosophers of his time, it is indisputable that his location in the Muslim-ruled area of what is now Spain meant that his works were primarily available thousands of miles west of the era&rsquo;s center of Islamic thought.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e85b0a0d-f31d-4e97-a809-56b35ebee3e9","content":"<p>controversial among</p>"},{"id":"3f2e8216-b231-41e6-a6a1-1501af42af48","content":"<p>antagonistic toward</p>"},{"id":"c848db49-6ac0-4f62-b04e-065d6aa16202","content":"<p>imitated by</p>"},{"id":"1d5f1a3d-84f1-4669-a345-0c4f7e8b4a72","content":"<p>inconsequential to</p>"}],"keys":["1d5f1a3d-84f1-4669-a345-0c4f7e8b4a72"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the twelfth-century Islamic philosopher Ibn Rushd. As used in this context, \"inconsequential to\" means not significant to. According to the text, Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works were primarily available in Spain, where he lived, far from central areas of the Muslim world, a fact that could support the conclusion that his influence on Muslim contemporaries was limited. The text implies, however, that recent scholarship has shown that his works still had an impact on other Muslim philosophers of his time. This context supports the idea that his works weren&rsquo;t inconsequential to Islamic thought in this period.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the issue under consideration in the text is whether other Muslim philosophers of Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s time had access to his works, not whether his works were \"controversial among,\" or causing dispute among, other philosophers. Choice B is incorrect. The text implies that other Muslim philosophers of Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s era were aware of his works, not that they were \"antagonistic toward,\" or hostile toward, them. There is no suggestion that Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s writings elicited hostility from his contemporaries. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"imitated by\" would mean followed as a model by. Although the text implies that Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works were at least somewhat available in regions that were at the center of Islamic thought during the period, it doesn&rsquo;t specifically address in what ways his works influenced contemporary Muslim philosophers. Thus, the text doesn&rsquo;t support the idea that other philosophers modeled their own works after Ibn Rushd&rsquo;s works.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323035,"updateDate":1730147323035}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323035, 1730147323035),
    ($SATQ$14b7dced$SATQ$, $SATQ$adce3048-dd82-4590-b1dd-157f352fe7d3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7d9eb205-ff97-455f-8e13-786d62871e1a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Walt Whitman&rsquo;s 1860 poem &ldquo;Calamus 24.&rdquo;</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">I HEAR it is charged against me that I seek to destroy institutions;</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">But really I am neither for nor against institutions</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">(What indeed have I in common with them?&mdash;Or what with the destruction of them?),</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">Only I will establish in the Mannahatta [Manhattan] and in every city of These States, inland and seaboard,</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">And in the fields and woods, and above every keel [ship] little or large, that dents the water,</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">Without edifices, or rules, or trustees, or any argument,</p><p style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;">The institution of the dear love of comrades.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d2a7f520-0787-4524-9796-724d12ad8d50","content":"<p>The speaker questions an increasingly prevalent attitude, then summarizes his worldview.</p>"},{"id":"9aadfce8-741c-4870-bf0f-bb821280a82b","content":"<p>The speaker regrets his isolation from others, then predicts a profound change in society.</p>"},{"id":"4982b64f-594b-49bf-80c3-cb3cf798a9db","content":"<p>The speaker concedes his personal shortcomings, then boasts of his many achievements.</p>"},{"id":"1de4ac13-b735-4bfa-ad00-99f5ed4c289b","content":"<p>The speaker addresses a criticism leveled against him, then announces a grand ambition of his.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1de4ac13-b735-4bfa-ad00-99f5ed4c289b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The speaker begins by stating that he has heard that others are accusing him of seeking to destroy institutions. The speaker then addresses this criticism by stating that he is &ldquo;neither for nor against institutions.&rdquo; Instead, the speaker states that his ultimate goal is to instill &ldquo;the institution of the dear love of comrades&rdquo; everywhere in the country. Therefore, the overall structure of the text is best described as an address of criticism followed by an announcement of a grand ambition. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the speaker does address an opinion of him that he believes to be untrue, he doesn&rsquo;t indicate that this attitude has become increasingly prevalent. The speaker also concludes by explaining his goal for the future rather than his current worldview. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the speaker as isolated or regretful, and the speaker gestures toward a hope for societal change but doesn&rsquo;t offer an explicit prediction that it will happen. Choice C is incorrect because the speaker addresses a criticism of him that he believes to be false; he doesn&rsquo;t admit any personal shortcomings. Moreover, the speaker concludes by stating a goal he has rather than showcasing his achievements. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-017-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$edacbd94-3dd5-4c4a-92d2-daa524252c7a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"14b7dced","external_id":"adce3048-dd82-4590-b1dd-157f352fe7d3","vaultid":"7d9eb205-ff97-455f-8e13-786d62871e1a","uId":"c7d24c9d-51e9-4df1-9751-908a625051bf","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Walt Whitman&rsquo;s 1860 poem &ldquo;Calamus 24.&rdquo;</p>\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">I HEAR it is charged against me that I seek to destroy institutions;</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">But really I am neither for nor against institutions</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">(What indeed have I in common with them?&mdash;Or what with the destruction of them?),</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">Only I will establish in the Mannahatta [Manhattan] and in every city of These States, inland and seaboard,</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">And in the fields and woods, and above every keel [ship] little or large, that dents the water,</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">Without edifices, or rules, or trustees, or any argument,</p><p style=\"margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 2em; text-indent: -1em;\">The institution of the dear love of comrades.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d2a7f520-0787-4524-9796-724d12ad8d50","content":"<p>The speaker questions an increasingly prevalent attitude, then summarizes his worldview.</p>"},{"id":"9aadfce8-741c-4870-bf0f-bb821280a82b","content":"<p>The speaker regrets his isolation from others, then predicts a profound change in society.</p>"},{"id":"4982b64f-594b-49bf-80c3-cb3cf798a9db","content":"<p>The speaker concedes his personal shortcomings, then boasts of his many achievements.</p>"},{"id":"1de4ac13-b735-4bfa-ad00-99f5ed4c289b","content":"<p>The speaker addresses a criticism leveled against him, then announces a grand ambition of his.</p>"}],"keys":["1de4ac13-b735-4bfa-ad00-99f5ed4c289b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The speaker begins by stating that he has heard that others are accusing him of seeking to destroy institutions. The speaker then addresses this criticism by stating that he is &ldquo;neither for nor against institutions.&rdquo; Instead, the speaker states that his ultimate goal is to instill &ldquo;the institution of the dear love of comrades&rdquo; everywhere in the country. Therefore, the overall structure of the text is best described as an address of criticism followed by an announcement of a grand ambition. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the speaker does address an opinion of him that he believes to be untrue, he doesn&rsquo;t indicate that this attitude has become increasingly prevalent. The speaker also concludes by explaining his goal for the future rather than his current worldview. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the speaker as isolated or regretful, and the speaker gestures toward a hope for societal change but doesn&rsquo;t offer an explicit prediction that it will happen. Choice C is incorrect because the speaker addresses a criticism of him that he believes to be false; he doesn&rsquo;t admit any personal shortcomings. Moreover, the speaker concludes by stating a goal he has rather than showcasing his achievements. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-017-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"edacbd94-3dd5-4c4a-92d2-daa524252c7a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959316,"updateDate":1691007959316}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959316, 1691007959316),
    ($SATQ$159ef46d$SATQ$, $SATQ$ec193c57-b35f-4cba-9b08-05153be0e012$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$35e778b7-aae2-4a9e-b5d8-81cbabbde453$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Although food writing is one of the most widely read genres in the United States, literary scholars have long neglected it. And within this genre, cookbooks attract the least scholarly attention of all, regardless of how well written they may be. This is especially true of works dedicated to regional US cuisines, whose complexity and historical significance are often overlooked.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>With her 1976 cookbook <em>The Taste of Country Cooking</em>, Edna Lewis popularized the refined Southern cooking she had grown up with in Freetown, an all-Black community in Virginia. She also set a new standard for cookbook writing: the recipes and memoir passages interspersing them are written in prose more elegant than that of most novels. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Yet despite its inarguable value as a piece of writing, Lewis&rsquo;s masterpiece has received almost no attention from literary scholars.</u></span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the two texts, how would the author of Text 1 most likely regard the situation presented in the underlined sentence in Text 2?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8419a8dd-7795-405f-a994-decac91592f1","content":"<p>As typical, because scholars are dismissive of literary works that achieve popularity with the general public</p>"},{"id":"1f9c8be3-9866-4cc8-9d48-fcef98bc6d70","content":"<p>As unsurprising, because scholars tend to overlook the literary value of food writing in general and of regional cookbooks in particular</p>"},{"id":"90edc05a-24ba-4549-b8db-247364871edb","content":"<p>As justifiable, because Lewis incorporated memoir into <em>The Taste of Country Cooking</em>, thus undermining its status as a cookbook</p>"},{"id":"0dd24cdd-8950-460d-9678-eed4d2bd970e","content":"<p>As inevitable, because <em>The Taste of Country Cooking </em>was marketed to readers of food writing and not to readers of other genres</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1f9c8be3-9866-4cc8-9d48-fcef98bc6d70"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Text 1 states that literary scholars ignore regional cookbooks most of all, even when they have historical significance and are well written. So the author of Text 1 wouldn&rsquo;t be surprised that scholars ignored Edna Lewis&rsquo;s cookbook. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never suggests that scholars are dismissive of popular works in general. Instead, Text 1 says that scholars ignore food writing specifically, despite its popularity&mdash;and despite the fact that it can be historically significant and complex. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never suggests that elements of other genres should be kept out of cookbooks. Choice D is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never discusses how food writing is or should be marketed. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-M002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$54d48ff0-c4f3-485b-a7ec-87b3455d7bc5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"159ef46d","external_id":"ec193c57-b35f-4cba-9b08-05153be0e012","vaultid":"35e778b7-aae2-4a9e-b5d8-81cbabbde453","uId":"d47ed95f-658d-44e9-b6d1-5ac176a748b1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Although food writing is one of the most widely read genres in the United States, literary scholars have long neglected it. And within this genre, cookbooks attract the least scholarly attention of all, regardless of how well written they may be. This is especially true of works dedicated to regional US cuisines, whose complexity and historical significance are often overlooked.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>With her 1976 cookbook <em>The Taste of Country Cooking</em>, Edna Lewis popularized the refined Southern cooking she had grown up with in Freetown, an all-Black community in Virginia. She also set a new standard for cookbook writing: the recipes and memoir passages interspersing them are written in prose more elegant than that of most novels. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Yet despite its inarguable value as a piece of writing, Lewis&rsquo;s masterpiece has received almost no attention from literary scholars.</u></span></p>","stem":"<p>Based on the two texts, how would the author of Text 1 most likely regard the situation presented in the underlined sentence in Text 2?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8419a8dd-7795-405f-a994-decac91592f1","content":"<p>As typical, because scholars are dismissive of literary works that achieve popularity with the general public</p>"},{"id":"1f9c8be3-9866-4cc8-9d48-fcef98bc6d70","content":"<p>As unsurprising, because scholars tend to overlook the literary value of food writing in general and of regional cookbooks in particular</p>"},{"id":"90edc05a-24ba-4549-b8db-247364871edb","content":"<p>As justifiable, because Lewis incorporated memoir into <em>The Taste of Country Cooking</em>, thus undermining its status as a cookbook</p>"},{"id":"0dd24cdd-8950-460d-9678-eed4d2bd970e","content":"<p>As inevitable, because <em>The Taste of Country Cooking </em>was marketed to readers of food writing and not to readers of other genres</p>"}],"keys":["1f9c8be3-9866-4cc8-9d48-fcef98bc6d70"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Text 1 states that literary scholars ignore regional cookbooks most of all, even when they have historical significance and are well written. So the author of Text 1 wouldn&rsquo;t be surprised that scholars ignored Edna Lewis&rsquo;s cookbook. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never suggests that scholars are dismissive of popular works in general. Instead, Text 1 says that scholars ignore food writing specifically, despite its popularity&mdash;and despite the fact that it can be historically significant and complex. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never suggests that elements of other genres should be kept out of cookbooks. Choice D is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that this is how the author of Text 1 would regard the situation. Text 1 never discusses how food writing is or should be marketed. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-M002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"54d48ff0-c4f3-485b-a7ec-87b3455d7bc5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959366,"updateDate":1691007959366}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959366, 1691007959366),
    ($SATQ$1695391a$SATQ$, $SATQ$5ef55ec1-fac7-4795-a283-c2458b390a51$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Alice Kaplan&rsquo;s 1993 memoir <em>French Lessons</em>.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When I started to talk on my own, <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped.</span> When I was in first grade, my sister&rsquo;s friends could hardly stand to ride to school with me in the car. I was loud and unrelenting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;1993 by The University of Chicago</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the author most nearly mean by &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo;?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"83956e59-506a-43a8-8045-57a73dd63840","content":"<p>She had a hard time sitting still.</p>"},{"id":"7f2c8956-1097-4d62-ad46-d4acd4c9ccdc","content":"<p>She often changed her mind.</p>"},{"id":"b86ae7cb-c312-4a38-baf9-45acde5cc515","content":"<p>She was well liked as a child.</p>"},{"id":"76371d2f-f26a-4b93-8bf9-6ad8ea230bef","content":"<p>She seemed to talk all the time.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["76371d2f-f26a-4b93-8bf9-6ad8ea230bef"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; most nearly means the author seemed to talk all the time. The text establishes that the author is discussing her early speaking habits, stating, &ldquo;When I started to talk on my own, I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped.&rdquo; The following sentences provide context that clarifies the significance of this statement: her sister&rsquo;s friends &ldquo;could hardly stand to ride to school&rdquo; with the author because she &ldquo;was loud and unrelenting.&rdquo; This suggests that once the author began speaking, she talked all the time&mdash;to the point of annoying others.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because although the phrase &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; could potentially refer to physical movement, the context clearly establishes that the author is discussing her talking habits, not her inability to sit still. The text specifically mentions that she &ldquo;was loud and unrelenting&rdquo; and that her sister&rsquo;s friends disliked riding in the car with her, suggesting that the issue was excessive talking, not physical restlessness. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the author was fickle or frequently changed her mind. In this context, the phrase &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; refers to the author&rsquo;s persistent talking, as signaled by her characterization as &ldquo;loud and unrelenting.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the author was popular or well liked as a child. In fact, it suggests the opposite by stating that her sister&rsquo;s friends &ldquo;could hardly stand&rdquo; to ride in the car with her. Because of her excessive talking, in other words, people found the author annoying as a child.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1695391a","external_id":"5ef55ec1-fac7-4795-a283-c2458b390a51","vaultid":null,"uId":"831f1fa8-cf55-401b-bc8c-e361e5857874","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Alice Kaplan&rsquo;s 1993 memoir <em>French Lessons</em>.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When I started to talk on my own, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped.</span> When I was in first grade, my sister&rsquo;s friends could hardly stand to ride to school with me in the car. I was loud and unrelenting.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;1993 by The University of Chicago</span></p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the author most nearly mean by &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo;?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"83956e59-506a-43a8-8045-57a73dd63840","content":"<p>She had a hard time sitting still.</p>"},{"id":"7f2c8956-1097-4d62-ad46-d4acd4c9ccdc","content":"<p>She often changed her mind.</p>"},{"id":"b86ae7cb-c312-4a38-baf9-45acde5cc515","content":"<p>She was well liked as a child.</p>"},{"id":"76371d2f-f26a-4b93-8bf9-6ad8ea230bef","content":"<p>She seemed to talk all the time.</p>"}],"keys":["76371d2f-f26a-4b93-8bf9-6ad8ea230bef"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; most nearly means the author seemed to talk all the time. The text establishes that the author is discussing her early speaking habits, stating, &ldquo;When I started to talk on my own, I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped.&rdquo; The following sentences provide context that clarifies the significance of this statement: her sister&rsquo;s friends &ldquo;could hardly stand to ride to school&rdquo; with the author because she &ldquo;was loud and unrelenting.&rdquo; This suggests that once the author began speaking, she talked all the time&mdash;to the point of annoying others.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because although the phrase &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; could potentially refer to physical movement, the context clearly establishes that the author is discussing her talking habits, not her inability to sit still. The text specifically mentions that she &ldquo;was loud and unrelenting&rdquo; and that her sister&rsquo;s friends disliked riding in the car with her, suggesting that the issue was excessive talking, not physical restlessness. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the author was fickle or frequently changed her mind. In this context, the phrase &ldquo;I couldn&rsquo;t be stopped&rdquo; refers to the author&rsquo;s persistent talking, as signaled by her characterization as &ldquo;loud and unrelenting.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the author was popular or well liked as a child. In fact, it suggests the opposite by stating that her sister&rsquo;s friends &ldquo;could hardly stand&rdquo; to ride in the car with her. Because of her excessive talking, in other words, people found the author annoying as a child.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771168,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771168, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$16f2d678$SATQ$, $SATQ$a5de0431-b778-4957-aae9-f107e1a2ab49$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Although the government of the Soviet Union attempted to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Georgi Vladimov&rsquo;s novel <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>, copies of the book circulated in secret among readers in several parts of the country.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ca81d4df-45fc-4a53-a90d-45d198502cd6","content":"<p>replicate</p>"},{"id":"312ed3ea-c851-4c6b-a772-eff84327b3c5","content":"<p>critique</p>"},{"id":"2d9e09f8-a102-436f-b66e-2d179ad1d6fc","content":"<p>import</p>"},{"id":"5ec3b89e-229a-47b1-8aa8-b4ac3345b222","content":"<p>suppress</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5ec3b89e-229a-47b1-8aa8-b4ac3345b222"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Georgi Vladimov&rsquo;s novel <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>. In this context, &ldquo;suppress&rdquo; means to prevent something, such as a book, from being accessed by the public. The text indicates that the novel was circulated secretly within the Soviet Union, a stratagem that was necessary because of the government&rsquo;s efforts to suppress the book, or prevent it from being circulated and read.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;replicate&rdquo; would mean to copy or reproduce a book, and this meaning wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>. If the Soviet government had attempted to replicate Vladimov&rsquo;s novel, there would be no reason for copies to circulate &ldquo;in secret,&rdquo; since the government would be actively making more copies available, not restricting access. Choice B is incorrect. Although a repressive government might well &ldquo;critique&rdquo; a novel, or evaluate it negatively, simply critiquing one wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily lead to copies of it circulating &ldquo;in secret.&rdquo; Instead, the text suggests that the Soviet government took decisive action against <em>Faithful Ruslan</em> that necessitated its underground circulation and did not merely offer criticism of the novel. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;import&rdquo; means to bring a product, such as a book, into a country from elsewhere. However, the text suggests that copies of <em>Faithful Ruslan</em> were already present in the Soviet Union and that the government was trying to restrict their circulation rather than increasing access to the novel by importing copies from another country.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"16f2d678","external_id":"a5de0431-b778-4957-aae9-f107e1a2ab49","vaultid":null,"uId":"a7fbcf87-aa73-45c4-8c33-8da73c9b48ea","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Although the government of the Soviet Union attempted to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Georgi Vladimov&rsquo;s novel <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>, copies of the book circulated in secret among readers in several parts of the country.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ca81d4df-45fc-4a53-a90d-45d198502cd6","content":"<p>replicate</p>"},{"id":"312ed3ea-c851-4c6b-a772-eff84327b3c5","content":"<p>critique</p>"},{"id":"2d9e09f8-a102-436f-b66e-2d179ad1d6fc","content":"<p>import</p>"},{"id":"5ec3b89e-229a-47b1-8aa8-b4ac3345b222","content":"<p>suppress</p>"}],"keys":["5ec3b89e-229a-47b1-8aa8-b4ac3345b222"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Georgi Vladimov&rsquo;s novel <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>. In this context, &ldquo;suppress&rdquo; means to prevent something, such as a book, from being accessed by the public. The text indicates that the novel was circulated secretly within the Soviet Union, a stratagem that was necessary because of the government&rsquo;s efforts to suppress the book, or prevent it from being circulated and read.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;replicate&rdquo; would mean to copy or reproduce a book, and this meaning wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em>Faithful Ruslan</em>. If the Soviet government had attempted to replicate Vladimov&rsquo;s novel, there would be no reason for copies to circulate &ldquo;in secret,&rdquo; since the government would be actively making more copies available, not restricting access. Choice B is incorrect. Although a repressive government might well &ldquo;critique&rdquo; a novel, or evaluate it negatively, simply critiquing one wouldn&rsquo;t necessarily lead to copies of it circulating &ldquo;in secret.&rdquo; Instead, the text suggests that the Soviet government took decisive action against <em>Faithful Ruslan</em> that necessitated its underground circulation and did not merely offer criticism of the novel. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;import&rdquo; means to bring a product, such as a book, into a country from elsewhere. However, the text suggests that copies of <em>Faithful Ruslan</em> were already present in the Soviet Union and that the government was trying to restrict their circulation rather than increasing access to the novel by importing copies from another country.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$1782cdd7$SATQ$, $SATQ$30636fa0-19a8-4ebe-b3f6-dfefc2c67f6c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ee732570-b5d8-4686-b5ea-b8d4e1334079$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In many agricultural environments, the banks of streams are kept forested to protect water quality, but it&rsquo;s been unclear what effects these forests may have on stream biodiversity. To investigate the issue, biologist Xingli Giam and colleagues studied an Indonesian oil palm plantation, comparing the species richness of forested streams with that of nonforested streams. Giam and colleagues found that species richness was significantly higher in forested streams, a finding the researchers attribute to the role leaf litter plays in sheltering fish from predators and providing food resources.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1f52af16-3fd9-4a1e-9893-c2a0b53dd3ab","content":"<p>It discusses research intended to settle a debate about how agricultural yields can be increased without negative effects on water quality. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"59c61ff5-7010-443f-a415-4bdfd1dca972","content":"<p>It explains the differences between stream-protection strategies used in oil palm plantations and stream-protection strategies used in other kinds of agricultural environments.</p>"},{"id":"3fb78bb3-9312-4280-a2dc-ae265fd4c753","content":"<p>It describes findings that challenge a previously held view about how fish that inhabit streams in agricultural environments attempt to avoid predators.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e13dd730-8925-443c-9695-d9f982c4cdef","content":"<p>It presents a study that addresses an unresolved question about the presence of forests along streams in agricultural environments.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e13dd730-8925-443c-9695-d9f982c4cdef"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The author first describes an unresolved question: what effect do bank forests have on stream biodiversity? Then the author presents a study that answers the question: bank forests increase stream biodiversity. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions agricultural yields. Choice B is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions other kinds of agricultural environments. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions any previously held view about how fish in these streams try to avoid predators. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c8d8aee6-a589-4282-9fc2-166d241dd84b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1782cdd7","external_id":"30636fa0-19a8-4ebe-b3f6-dfefc2c67f6c","vaultid":"ee732570-b5d8-4686-b5ea-b8d4e1334079","uId":"cc760895-edda-480b-bf75-c3910b4efcd9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In many agricultural environments, the banks of streams are kept forested to protect water quality, but it&rsquo;s been unclear what effects these forests may have on stream biodiversity. To investigate the issue, biologist Xingli Giam and colleagues studied an Indonesian oil palm plantation, comparing the species richness of forested streams with that of nonforested streams. Giam and colleagues found that species richness was significantly higher in forested streams, a finding the researchers attribute to the role leaf litter plays in sheltering fish from predators and providing food resources.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1f52af16-3fd9-4a1e-9893-c2a0b53dd3ab","content":"<p>It discusses research intended to settle a debate about how agricultural yields can be increased without negative effects on water quality. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"59c61ff5-7010-443f-a415-4bdfd1dca972","content":"<p>It explains the differences between stream-protection strategies used in oil palm plantations and stream-protection strategies used in other kinds of agricultural environments.</p>"},{"id":"3fb78bb3-9312-4280-a2dc-ae265fd4c753","content":"<p>It describes findings that challenge a previously held view about how fish that inhabit streams in agricultural environments attempt to avoid predators.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"e13dd730-8925-443c-9695-d9f982c4cdef","content":"<p>It presents a study that addresses an unresolved question about the presence of forests along streams in agricultural environments.</p>"}],"keys":["e13dd730-8925-443c-9695-d9f982c4cdef"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The author first describes an unresolved question: what effect do bank forests have on stream biodiversity? Then the author presents a study that answers the question: bank forests increase stream biodiversity. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions agricultural yields. Choice B is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions other kinds of agricultural environments. Choice C is incorrect. This isn&rsquo;t the main purpose of the text. The text never mentions any previously held view about how fish in these streams try to avoid predators. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c8d8aee6-a589-4282-9fc2-166d241dd84b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$17bf10de$SATQ$, $SATQ$a064e000-78cc-447e-93c8-47f55b155b7b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6c29c6fe-620c-4cf5-a3e0-94600e9ff187$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong><span role="heading" aria-level="2">Text 1</span></strong></p>
<p>Despite its beautiful prose, <em>The Guns of August</em>, Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s 1962 analysis of the start of World War I, has certain weaknesses as a work of history. It fails to address events in Eastern Europe just before the outbreak of hostilities, thereby giving the impression that Germany was the war&rsquo;s principal instigator. Had Tuchman consulted secondary works available to her by scholars such as Luigi Albertini, she would not have neglected the influence of events in Eastern Europe on Germany&rsquo;s actions.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong><span role="heading" aria-level="2">Text 2</span></strong></p>
<p>Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s <em>The Guns of August </em>is an engrossing if dated introduction to World War I. Tuchman&rsquo;s analysis of primary documents is laudable, but her main thesis that European powers committed themselves to a catastrophic outcome by refusing to deviate from military plans developed prior to the conflict is implausibly reductive.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s <em>The Guns of August</em>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"911eb1e4-471f-4313-bb4b-5fccb04bc3b7","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 argues that Tuchman should have relied more on the work of other historians, while the author of Text 2 implies that Tuchman&rsquo;s most interesting claims result from her original research.</p>"},{"id":"91c8ded8-75ad-4e79-9154-b51a1e1b256e","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 believes that the scope of Tuchman&rsquo;s research led her to an incorrect interpretation, while the author of Text 2 believes that Tuchman&rsquo;s central argument is overly simplistic.</p>"},{"id":"096102df-7111-47ca-af93-00a4ffb67812","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 asserts that the writing style of <em>The Guns of August</em> makes it worthwhile to read despite any perceived deficiency in Tuchman&rsquo;s research, while the author of Text 2 focuses exclusively on the weakness of Tuchman&rsquo;s interpretation of events.</p>"},{"id":"72a712de-2d8b-41b3-8e24-8ed305643daf","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 claims that Tuchman would agree that World War I was largely due to events in Eastern Europe, while the author of Text 2 maintains that Tuchman would say that Eastern European leaders were not committed to military plans in the same way that other leaders were.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["91c8ded8-75ad-4e79-9154-b51a1e1b256e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both texts are critical of <em>The Guns of August</em>, but for different reasons: the author of Text 1 argues that Tuchman missed an important factor leading up to the war because she didn&rsquo;t consult secondary sources, and the author of Text 2 argues that Tuchman&rsquo;s main thesis is "reductive," which is a close synonym for "overly simplistic."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. This choice&rsquo;s summary of Text 1 is accurate, but Text 2 never says that Tuchman&rsquo;s most interesting claims result from her original research. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. Text 1 never says that <em>The Guns of August</em> is worthwhile to read despite its research weaknesses. Text 2 does call out a weakness of Tuchman&rsquo;s interpretation of events, but it also praises her analysis of primary sources. Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. Text 1 actually says that Tuchman "fails to address" the influence of events in Eastern Europe, while Text 2 says that Tuchman&rsquo;s thesis was that European powers (not Eastern European leaders) were committed to military plans.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8b2ec619-ef55-4cbb-87dc-bf6833be97bb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"17bf10de","external_id":"a064e000-78cc-447e-93c8-47f55b155b7b","vaultid":"6c29c6fe-620c-4cf5-a3e0-94600e9ff187","uId":"bea8b277-d515-4820-afa4-8e80ade27ecc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\">Text 1</span></strong></p>\n<p>Despite its beautiful prose, <em>The Guns of August</em>, Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s 1962 analysis of the start of World War I, has certain weaknesses as a work of history. It fails to address events in Eastern Europe just before the outbreak of hostilities, thereby giving the impression that Germany was the war&rsquo;s principal instigator. Had Tuchman consulted secondary works available to her by scholars such as Luigi Albertini, she would not have neglected the influence of events in Eastern Europe on Germany&rsquo;s actions.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\">Text 2</span></strong></p>\n<p>Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s <em>The Guns of August </em>is an engrossing if dated introduction to World War I. Tuchman&rsquo;s analysis of primary documents is laudable, but her main thesis that European powers committed themselves to a catastrophic outcome by refusing to deviate from military plans developed prior to the conflict is implausibly reductive.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view Barbara Tuchman&rsquo;s <em>The Guns of August</em>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"911eb1e4-471f-4313-bb4b-5fccb04bc3b7","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 argues that Tuchman should have relied more on the work of other historians, while the author of Text 2 implies that Tuchman&rsquo;s most interesting claims result from her original research.</p>"},{"id":"91c8ded8-75ad-4e79-9154-b51a1e1b256e","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 believes that the scope of Tuchman&rsquo;s research led her to an incorrect interpretation, while the author of Text 2 believes that Tuchman&rsquo;s central argument is overly simplistic.</p>"},{"id":"096102df-7111-47ca-af93-00a4ffb67812","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 asserts that the writing style of <em>The Guns of August</em> makes it worthwhile to read despite any perceived deficiency in Tuchman&rsquo;s research, while the author of Text 2 focuses exclusively on the weakness of Tuchman&rsquo;s interpretation of events.</p>"},{"id":"72a712de-2d8b-41b3-8e24-8ed305643daf","content":"<p>The author of Text 1 claims that Tuchman would agree that World War I was largely due to events in Eastern Europe, while the author of Text 2 maintains that Tuchman would say that Eastern European leaders were not committed to military plans in the same way that other leaders were.</p>"}],"keys":["91c8ded8-75ad-4e79-9154-b51a1e1b256e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both texts are critical of <em>The Guns of August</em>, but for different reasons: the author of Text 1 argues that Tuchman missed an important factor leading up to the war because she didn&rsquo;t consult secondary sources, and the author of Text 2 argues that Tuchman&rsquo;s main thesis is \"reductive,\" which is a close synonym for \"overly simplistic.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. This choice&rsquo;s summary of Text 1 is accurate, but Text 2 never says that Tuchman&rsquo;s most interesting claims result from her original research. Choice C is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. Text 1 never says that <em>The Guns of August</em> is worthwhile to read despite its research weaknesses. Text 2 does call out a weakness of Tuchman&rsquo;s interpretation of events, but it also praises her analysis of primary sources. Choice D is incorrect. This doesn&rsquo;t accurately describe the difference. Text 1 actually says that Tuchman \"fails to address\" the influence of events in Eastern Europe, while Text 2 says that Tuchman&rsquo;s thesis was that European powers (not Eastern European leaders) were committed to military plans.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8b2ec619-ef55-4cbb-87dc-bf6833be97bb","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$190857f0$SATQ$, $SATQ$8656a5f1-3a91-418f-81e9-f4fbc3a8e978$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Why do sand cats purr but lions roar? Researchers hypothesize that this difference between the two feline species may be partly due to a U-shaped bone in their throats called the hyoid. Sand cats, which are much smaller than lions, have a rigid hyoid that rumbles when the cat&rsquo;s larynx vibrates, resulting in a purr. By contrast, lions have a somewhat flexible hyoid, and the bone is attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that sand cats lack. These traits allow lions and most other species of big cats to produce powerful roars. The same traits may also prevent most big cats from purring.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2a067bf3-b253-4f26-9268-0e4449b190d9","content":"<p>The text presents a theory about two species, then discusses facts that weaken it.</p>"},{"id":"7438a279-c0b3-4b7d-bf21-7dd27c8202ff","content":"<p>The text compares the habitats of two species, then explains how those habitats are changing.</p>"},{"id":"7f622702-2c61-4148-8136-554f5260058a","content":"<p>The text describes a behavior shared by two species, then discusses other behaviors shared by them.</p>"},{"id":"68cc412c-b94f-4d81-a02c-7f72f0915d9c","content":"<p>The text poses a question about two species, then presents a possible answer.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["68cc412c-b94f-4d81-a02c-7f72f0915d9c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by posing a question about two different cat species: "Why do sand cats purr but lions roar?" The remainder of the text then presents a possible answer to this question by explaining a hypothesis about differences in these cats&rsquo; hyoid bone structure. The text details how sand cats have rigid hyoids that produce purring sounds when their larynx vibrates, while lions have more flexible hyoids attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that enables roaring but may prevent purring.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present facts that weaken the theory about two cat species; rather, it asks a question about two species and then discusses a potential answer. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the habitats of sand cats and lions at all, nor does it mention any changes to their habitats. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe a behavior shared by the two cat species mentioned. On the contrary, it specifically focuses on the different vocalization behaviors of each species (purring versus roaring) and why those differences may exist.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"190857f0","external_id":"8656a5f1-3a91-418f-81e9-f4fbc3a8e978","vaultid":null,"uId":"2e248540-d692-48d7-a8e8-c5f17ef896ad","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Why do sand cats purr but lions roar? Researchers hypothesize that this difference between the two feline species may be partly due to a U-shaped bone in their throats called the hyoid. Sand cats, which are much smaller than lions, have a rigid hyoid that rumbles when the cat&rsquo;s larynx vibrates, resulting in a purr. By contrast, lions have a somewhat flexible hyoid, and the bone is attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that sand cats lack. These traits allow lions and most other species of big cats to produce powerful roars. The same traits may also prevent most big cats from purring.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2a067bf3-b253-4f26-9268-0e4449b190d9","content":"<p>The text presents a theory about two species, then discusses facts that weaken it.</p>"},{"id":"7438a279-c0b3-4b7d-bf21-7dd27c8202ff","content":"<p>The text compares the habitats of two species, then explains how those habitats are changing.</p>"},{"id":"7f622702-2c61-4148-8136-554f5260058a","content":"<p>The text describes a behavior shared by two species, then discusses other behaviors shared by them.</p>"},{"id":"68cc412c-b94f-4d81-a02c-7f72f0915d9c","content":"<p>The text poses a question about two species, then presents a possible answer.</p>"}],"keys":["68cc412c-b94f-4d81-a02c-7f72f0915d9c"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by posing a question about two different cat species: \"Why do sand cats purr but lions roar?\" The remainder of the text then presents a possible answer to this question by explaining a hypothesis about differences in these cats&rsquo; hyoid bone structure. The text details how sand cats have rigid hyoids that produce purring sounds when their larynx vibrates, while lions have more flexible hyoids attached to the skull with a stretchy ligament that enables roaring but may prevent purring.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present facts that weaken the theory about two cat species; rather, it asks a question about two species and then discusses a potential answer. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the habitats of sand cats and lions at all, nor does it mention any changes to their habitats. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe a behavior shared by the two cat species mentioned. On the contrary, it specifically focuses on the different vocalization behaviors of each species (purring versus roaring) and why those differences may exist.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061189,"updateDate":1755117061189}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061189, 1755117061189),
    ($SATQ$19688783$SATQ$, $SATQ$6e88aa39-968c-4603-8ae8-3ec568e6fc0b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$aef6d76c-9d2a-48d6-99c3-603ad9844e7b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Lucy Maud Montgomery&rsquo;s 1908 novel <em>Anne of Green Gables</em>. Anne, an eleven-year-old girl, has come to live on a farm with a woman named Marilla in Nova Scotia, Canada.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&nbsp;Anne reveled in the world of color about her.</p>
<p>&ldquo;Oh, Marilla,&rdquo; she exclaimed one Saturday morning, coming dancing in with her arms full of gorgeous boughs, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m so glad I live in a world where there are Octobers. It would be terrible if we just skipped from September to November, wouldn&rsquo;t it? Look at these maple branches. Don&rsquo;t they give you a thrill&mdash;several thrills? I&rsquo;m going to decorate my room with them.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;Messy things,&rdquo; said Marilla, whose aesthetic sense was not noticeably developed. &ldquo;You clutter up your room entirely too much with out-of-doors stuff, Anne. Bedrooms were made to sleep in.&rdquo;</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"39736276-fbc7-4e8d-97ad-5c22324f8f68","content":"<p>To demonstrate that Anne has a newly developed appreciation of nature</p>"},{"id":"42646366-f0d8-4fcd-bf80-5f99bb2d2c2f","content":"<p>To describe an argument that Anne and Marilla often have</p>"},{"id":"0c6f3e08-f431-4c22-9b52-20b72c76c156","content":"<p>To emphasize Marilla&rsquo;s disapproval of how Anne has decorated her room</p>"},{"id":"bfdb9239-c491-4297-b1f4-ff7380ce56d0","content":"<p>To show that Anne and Marilla have very different personalities</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bfdb9239-c491-4297-b1f4-ff7380ce56d0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text begins by noting that Anne &ldquo;reveled in the world of color about her&rdquo;&mdash;that is, she takes great delight in colorful things. It then relates a scene when she enthusiastically enters the house with autumn foliage and announces that she will decorate her room with it. The focus of the text then shifts to Marilla, who has an undeveloped &ldquo;aesthetic sense,&rdquo; or appreciation of beauty, as can be seen when she dismisses the maple leaves as &ldquo;messy things&rdquo; and criticizes Anne for cluttering her room with objects from outside. This episode thus illustrates that Anne and Marilla differ in their appreciation of beauty and, more generally, in their basic character: Anne is exuberant and joyful, while Marilla is stern and critical. Therefore, the purpose of the text is to show that Anne and Marilla have very different personalities. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text presents Anne&rsquo;s appreciation of nature as a basic personality trait, not as a newfound enthusiasm, and never indicates how recently she developed that appreciation. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text portrays Anne and Marilla as having different personalities and attitudes toward natural beauty and home decoration, it doesn&rsquo;t show them engaging in an argument about this difference or suggest that they often argue about it. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text does indicate that Marilla disapproves of how Anne plans to decorate her room, Marilla&rsquo;s disapproval is a supporting detail that serves to develop her personality, which the text as a whole contrasts with Anne&rsquo;s personality. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-023-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$769ecf2d-4b5a-4b99-9eb4-ea4f7c76301c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"19688783","external_id":"6e88aa39-968c-4603-8ae8-3ec568e6fc0b","vaultid":"aef6d76c-9d2a-48d6-99c3-603ad9844e7b","uId":"cde9a15d-94aa-427a-a266-4dc5e6fda272","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Lucy Maud Montgomery&rsquo;s 1908 novel <em>Anne of Green Gables</em>. Anne, an eleven-year-old girl, has come to live on a farm with a woman named Marilla in Nova Scotia, Canada.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;Anne reveled in the world of color about her.</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Oh, Marilla,&rdquo; she exclaimed one Saturday morning, coming dancing in with her arms full of gorgeous boughs, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m so glad I live in a world where there are Octobers. It would be terrible if we just skipped from September to November, wouldn&rsquo;t it? Look at these maple branches. Don&rsquo;t they give you a thrill&mdash;several thrills? I&rsquo;m going to decorate my room with them.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Messy things,&rdquo; said Marilla, whose aesthetic sense was not noticeably developed. &ldquo;You clutter up your room entirely too much with out-of-doors stuff, Anne. Bedrooms were made to sleep in.&rdquo;</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"39736276-fbc7-4e8d-97ad-5c22324f8f68","content":"<p>To demonstrate that Anne has a newly developed appreciation of nature</p>"},{"id":"42646366-f0d8-4fcd-bf80-5f99bb2d2c2f","content":"<p>To describe an argument that Anne and Marilla often have</p>"},{"id":"0c6f3e08-f431-4c22-9b52-20b72c76c156","content":"<p>To emphasize Marilla&rsquo;s disapproval of how Anne has decorated her room</p>"},{"id":"bfdb9239-c491-4297-b1f4-ff7380ce56d0","content":"<p>To show that Anne and Marilla have very different personalities</p>"}],"keys":["bfdb9239-c491-4297-b1f4-ff7380ce56d0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text begins by noting that Anne &ldquo;reveled in the world of color about her&rdquo;&mdash;that is, she takes great delight in colorful things. It then relates a scene when she enthusiastically enters the house with autumn foliage and announces that she will decorate her room with it. The focus of the text then shifts to Marilla, who has an undeveloped &ldquo;aesthetic sense,&rdquo; or appreciation of beauty, as can be seen when she dismisses the maple leaves as &ldquo;messy things&rdquo; and criticizes Anne for cluttering her room with objects from outside. This episode thus illustrates that Anne and Marilla differ in their appreciation of beauty and, more generally, in their basic character: Anne is exuberant and joyful, while Marilla is stern and critical. Therefore, the purpose of the text is to show that Anne and Marilla have very different personalities. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text presents Anne&rsquo;s appreciation of nature as a basic personality trait, not as a newfound enthusiasm, and never indicates how recently she developed that appreciation. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text portrays Anne and Marilla as having different personalities and attitudes toward natural beauty and home decoration, it doesn&rsquo;t show them engaging in an argument about this difference or suggest that they often argue about it. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text does indicate that Marilla disapproves of how Anne plans to decorate her room, Marilla&rsquo;s disapproval is a supporting detail that serves to develop her personality, which the text as a whole contrasts with Anne&rsquo;s personality. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-023-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"769ecf2d-4b5a-4b99-9eb4-ea4f7c76301c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$1ad04ea0$SATQ$, $SATQ$ea2589eb-9e1f-4005-a0c7-0b5721317471$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3c2189b4-4391-4445-b557-0d6c182c213a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In habitats with limited nutrients, certain fungus species grow on the roots of trees, engaging in mutually beneficial relationships known as ectomycorrhizae: in this symbiotic exchange, the tree provides the fungus with carbon, a nutrient necessary for both species, and the fungus <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> by enhancing the tree&rsquo;s ability to absorb nitrogen, another key nutrient, from the soil.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3208ecc7-1eb8-462d-9d99-6d9dd3b8690f","content":"<p>overreacts</p>"},{"id":"d6908c5e-d019-4db5-ad93-4e7644ad41c4","content":"<p>reciprocates</p>"},{"id":"6eba76cc-5ba6-4a3c-ac1a-3742f9b1f5ab","content":"<p>retaliates</p>"},{"id":"6bc14c62-5b82-45cb-b398-2eb5bdf1f6ee","content":"<p>deviates</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d6908c5e-d019-4db5-ad93-4e7644ad41c4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of ectomycorrhizae relationships. In this context, &ldquo;reciprocates&rdquo; means responds in kind or degree. The text indicates that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats is &ldquo;mutually beneficial&rdquo; and involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. In other words, each organism provides the same kind of benefit it receives: the tree provides a nutrient (carbon) for the fungus and the fungus reciprocates by helping the tree to absorb more of another nutrient (nitrogen). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text emphasizes that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. Nothing in the text suggests that the fungus &ldquo;overreacts,&rdquo; or responds too strongly, by allowing the tree to be better able to absorb a beneficial nutrient. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;retaliates&rdquo; means responds to a harmful action with a similarly harmful action. The text indicates that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats is &ldquo;mutually beneficial&rdquo; and involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other, not that the relationship is one in which the organisms harm one another.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;deviates&rdquo; would mean departs from an established course or norm. The text explains that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. Because the relationship involves benefits for both the fungus and the tree, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the fungus deviates by helping the tree be better able to absorb a beneficial nutrient. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-959-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c7240a94-79f5-4df6-b3a4-cea5866acdc1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1ad04ea0","external_id":"ea2589eb-9e1f-4005-a0c7-0b5721317471","vaultid":"3c2189b4-4391-4445-b557-0d6c182c213a","uId":"775912ce-c008-4813-9d18-89a9d0b8b55b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In habitats with limited nutrients, certain fungus species grow on the roots of trees, engaging in mutually beneficial relationships known as ectomycorrhizae: in this symbiotic exchange, the tree provides the fungus with carbon, a nutrient necessary for both species, and the fungus <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> by enhancing the tree&rsquo;s ability to absorb nitrogen, another key nutrient, from the soil.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3208ecc7-1eb8-462d-9d99-6d9dd3b8690f","content":"<p>overreacts</p>"},{"id":"d6908c5e-d019-4db5-ad93-4e7644ad41c4","content":"<p>reciprocates</p>"},{"id":"6eba76cc-5ba6-4a3c-ac1a-3742f9b1f5ab","content":"<p>retaliates</p>"},{"id":"6bc14c62-5b82-45cb-b398-2eb5bdf1f6ee","content":"<p>deviates</p>"}],"keys":["d6908c5e-d019-4db5-ad93-4e7644ad41c4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of ectomycorrhizae relationships. In this context, &ldquo;reciprocates&rdquo; means responds in kind or degree. The text indicates that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats is &ldquo;mutually beneficial&rdquo; and involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. In other words, each organism provides the same kind of benefit it receives: the tree provides a nutrient (carbon) for the fungus and the fungus reciprocates by helping the tree to absorb more of another nutrient (nitrogen). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text emphasizes that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. Nothing in the text suggests that the fungus &ldquo;overreacts,&rdquo; or responds too strongly, by allowing the tree to be better able to absorb a beneficial nutrient. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;retaliates&rdquo; means responds to a harmful action with a similarly harmful action. The text indicates that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats is &ldquo;mutually beneficial&rdquo; and involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other, not that the relationship is one in which the organisms harm one another.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;deviates&rdquo; would mean departs from an established course or norm. The text explains that the relationship between certain fungi and trees in some habitats involves a &ldquo;symbiotic exchange&rdquo; in which each organism helps the other access an important nutrient. Because the relationship involves benefits for both the fungus and the tree, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that the fungus deviates by helping the tree be better able to absorb a beneficial nutrient. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-959-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c7240a94-79f5-4df6-b3a4-cea5866acdc1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$1b5d4e3e$SATQ$, $SATQ$16fda5ff-ff75-400f-aa99-6cbb9497d1be$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Were penguins always flightless? <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">Theresa Cole and her team argue that penguins could fly at some point, but that they lost that ability more than 60 million years ago as they adapted to marine life.</span> After examining various penguin fossils and genetic information, the researchers concluded that over time penguins developed underwater vision, blood oxygenation, and bone density better suited for swimming than flying. Thus, environmental conditions might have driven penguins to change from flyers to swimmers.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"23649a17-0abb-415d-b2d4-3eb2dad18f71","content":"<p>It defines a scientific term used in the sentence that follows.</p>"},{"id":"4bf1059f-49b3-4c4c-b4e7-a5b628c4d8b4","content":"<p>It contradicts a description in the sentence that follows.</p>"},{"id":"ee75dfc1-6957-408e-9a18-571e21bcf3e2","content":"<p>It provides an answer to the question in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"c3f13135-9731-4b7c-9703-83f490b3a8d8","content":"<p>It notes that the question in the previous sentence has not been researched.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ee75dfc1-6957-408e-9a18-571e21bcf3e2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The text begins with a question: have penguins always been unable to fly? The underlined sentence then presents an answer that has been offered by researchers, indicating that Theresa Cole and her team believe that penguins were once able to fly but lost that ability long ago as they began living in the sea. The text then describes the research that led to that answer. Thus, the function of the underlined sentence is to provide an answer to the question in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because none of the terms used in the sentence that follows are defined in the underlined sentence; no definitions are given at all. Instead, the sentence presents Theresa Cole and her team&rsquo;s answer to the earlier question about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly. Choice B is incorrect because there is no contradiction between the underlined sentence and the sentence that follows since both convey that at some point penguins underwent adaptations that limited their ability to fly and helped them swim and live in the sea. Instead of contradicting later information, the underlined sentence presents an answer to the earlier question about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly. Choice D is incorrect because the question in the previous sentence is about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly and the underlined sentence presents Theresa Cole and her team&rsquo;s answer to that question based on their research into penguin flight, which is described in the rest of the text.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1b5d4e3e","external_id":"16fda5ff-ff75-400f-aa99-6cbb9497d1be","vaultid":null,"uId":"8ae88d41-852c-442e-902f-de986c82588a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Were penguins always flightless? <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">Theresa Cole and her team argue that penguins could fly at some point, but that they lost that ability more than 60 million years ago as they adapted to marine life.</span> After examining various penguin fossils and genetic information, the researchers concluded that over time penguins developed underwater vision, blood oxygenation, and bone density better suited for swimming than flying. Thus, environmental conditions might have driven penguins to change from flyers to swimmers.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"23649a17-0abb-415d-b2d4-3eb2dad18f71","content":"<p>It defines a scientific term used in the sentence that follows.</p>"},{"id":"4bf1059f-49b3-4c4c-b4e7-a5b628c4d8b4","content":"<p>It contradicts a description in the sentence that follows.</p>"},{"id":"ee75dfc1-6957-408e-9a18-571e21bcf3e2","content":"<p>It provides an answer to the question in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"c3f13135-9731-4b7c-9703-83f490b3a8d8","content":"<p>It notes that the question in the previous sentence has not been researched.</p>"}],"keys":["ee75dfc1-6957-408e-9a18-571e21bcf3e2"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The text begins with a question: have penguins always been unable to fly? The underlined sentence then presents an answer that has been offered by researchers, indicating that Theresa Cole and her team believe that penguins were once able to fly but lost that ability long ago as they began living in the sea. The text then describes the research that led to that answer. Thus, the function of the underlined sentence is to provide an answer to the question in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because none of the terms used in the sentence that follows are defined in the underlined sentence; no definitions are given at all. Instead, the sentence presents Theresa Cole and her team&rsquo;s answer to the earlier question about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly. Choice B is incorrect because there is no contradiction between the underlined sentence and the sentence that follows since both convey that at some point penguins underwent adaptations that limited their ability to fly and helped them swim and live in the sea. Instead of contradicting later information, the underlined sentence presents an answer to the earlier question about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly. Choice D is incorrect because the question in the previous sentence is about penguins&rsquo; ability to fly and the underlined sentence presents Theresa Cole and her team&rsquo;s answer to that question based on their research into penguin flight, which is described in the rest of the text.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323036,"updateDate":1730147323036}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323036, 1730147323036),
    ($SATQ$1c6b1fa0$SATQ$, $SATQ$7c89e3ee-faaa-4316-9ca0-e0d2363b46c4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9d1d23fc-e4bd-468e-a820-f9fdd10164ca$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1801, a Blackfoot chief named Ac Ko Mok Ki drew a finely detailed map of the Upper Missouri region. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>This work demonstrates a vast amount of topographic knowledge, as the map features specific names of mountains and rivers, as well as the first-known sketch of the drainage network of the Missouri River.</u></span> The map is especially notable because Ac Ko Mok Ki also included details about the numerous tribes that lived in the area.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"e75e8079-b757-458e-b8a7-3e4a41158dba","content":"<p>It emphasizes Ac Ko Mok Ki&rsquo;s desire to represent other tribes on the map.</p>"},{"id":"79a03986-d90f-4418-bdd8-8e0c82f67d11","content":"<p>It explains how Ac Ko Mok Ki developed an interest in mapmaking.</p>"},{"id":"4194f6af-7355-4ddd-a0d6-93ff8b534344","content":"<p>It identifies some reasons why the map is impressive.</p>"},{"id":"20119afb-88cb-4fde-b07d-db95abdc4c08","content":"<p>It details how the map was used for hunting and trading purposes.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4194f6af-7355-4ddd-a0d6-93ff8b534344"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The text presents information about a map drawn by Blackfoot chief Ac Ko Mok Ki in 1801. The underlined sentence states that the map "demonstrates a vast amount of topographic knowledge" and mentions that it features the "specific names of mountains and rivers" and includes the first-known sketch of the Missouri River&rsquo;s drainage network. These are all characteristics that indicate that the map was executed with remarkable skill. Thus, the underlined sentence identifies some reasons why the map is impressive.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Though the sentence after the underlined sentence in the text mentions that Ac Ko Mok Ki included information about other tribes on his map, the underlined sentence itself does not address this topic. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the underlined sentence indicates how Ac Ko Mok Ki became interested in mapmaking, only that his mapmaking skills are impressive. Choice D is incorrect because though the underlined sentence describes several features of the map, it does not specifically describe how the map was used.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-039-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ee3ae562-3023-4fb8-a3a4-97c7f20f642b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1c6b1fa0","external_id":"7c89e3ee-faaa-4316-9ca0-e0d2363b46c4","vaultid":"9d1d23fc-e4bd-468e-a820-f9fdd10164ca","uId":"cba2a03d-01c9-484c-8444-e46e790572c1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1801, a Blackfoot chief named Ac Ko Mok Ki drew a finely detailed map of the Upper Missouri region. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>This work demonstrates a vast amount of topographic knowledge, as the map features specific names of mountains and rivers, as well as the first-known sketch of the drainage network of the Missouri River.</u></span> The map is especially notable because Ac Ko Mok Ki also included details about the numerous tribes that lived in the area.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"e75e8079-b757-458e-b8a7-3e4a41158dba","content":"<p>It emphasizes Ac Ko Mok Ki&rsquo;s desire to represent other tribes on the map.</p>"},{"id":"79a03986-d90f-4418-bdd8-8e0c82f67d11","content":"<p>It explains how Ac Ko Mok Ki developed an interest in mapmaking.</p>"},{"id":"4194f6af-7355-4ddd-a0d6-93ff8b534344","content":"<p>It identifies some reasons why the map is impressive.</p>"},{"id":"20119afb-88cb-4fde-b07d-db95abdc4c08","content":"<p>It details how the map was used for hunting and trading purposes.</p>"}],"keys":["4194f6af-7355-4ddd-a0d6-93ff8b534344"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The text presents information about a map drawn by Blackfoot chief Ac Ko Mok Ki in 1801. The underlined sentence states that the map \"demonstrates a vast amount of topographic knowledge\" and mentions that it features the \"specific names of mountains and rivers\" and includes the first-known sketch of the Missouri River&rsquo;s drainage network. These are all characteristics that indicate that the map was executed with remarkable skill. Thus, the underlined sentence identifies some reasons why the map is impressive.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Though the sentence after the underlined sentence in the text mentions that Ac Ko Mok Ki included information about other tribes on his map, the underlined sentence itself does not address this topic. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the underlined sentence indicates how Ac Ko Mok Ki became interested in mapmaking, only that his mapmaking skills are impressive. Choice D is incorrect because though the underlined sentence describes several features of the map, it does not specifically describe how the map was used.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-039-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ee3ae562-3023-4fb8-a3a4-97c7f20f642b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959321,"updateDate":1691007959321}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959321, 1691007959321),
    ($SATQ$1c7fe9be$SATQ$, $SATQ$295098a9-c956-4c70-8a57-bdfa82220574$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>At the turn of the twentieth century, Black residents of Richmond, Virginia, had few formal options for banking and other financial services. To <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> this situation, Maggie Lena Walker chartered the <span data-ssml-sub-alias="St">St.</span> Luke Penny Savings Bank in 1903. The bank went on to provide home loans and savings opportunities to thousands of Black families over the following decades.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"84edcdbe-6a57-4d70-9be5-b490a29a1f24","content":"<p>prolong</p>"},{"id":"7668009c-be8f-4783-839b-b6fbbda61b09","content":"<p>rectify</p>"},{"id":"835e78ac-c06f-42c0-9456-853b6bfa5030","content":"<p>retain</p>"},{"id":"e21fbf60-508f-48d9-9d72-c1341f65ee0e","content":"<p>highlight</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7668009c-be8f-4783-839b-b6fbbda61b09"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how Maggie Lena Walker addressed the lack of financial services available to Black residents in Richmond, Virginia, at the turn of the twentieth century. In this context, "rectify" means to correct or remedy something undesirable. The text indicates that by chartering the St. Luke Penny Savings Bank in 1903, Walker took action to provide local Black residents with greater access to financial services like home loans and savings opportunities. This context supports the idea that she aimed to rectify the undesirable situation affecting these residents.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "prolong" would mean to lengthen something in time. The text indicates that at the turn of the twentieth century, Black residents in Richmond, Virginia, were faced with a lack of formal banking options. The text then states that Walker founded a new bank that provided these residents with financial services. Therefore, instead of prolonging the situation, she took steps to rectify, or correct, it. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "retain" would mean to continue to have or to keep something. According to the text, at the turn of the twentieth century, Black residents in Richmond, Virginia, had few formal banking options, and Walker chartered a new institution to provide these residents with expanded financial services; therefore, she took steps to rectify, not retain, the situation. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "highlight" would mean to emphasize or call attention to something, but the text indicates that Walker took concrete steps beyond merely drawing attention to the situation Black residents were facing in Richmond, Virginia, at the turn of the twentieth century. According to the text, Walker worked to rectify, or correct, the lack of formal banking options that were available to these residents by establishing a bank that provided them with home loans and savings opportunities.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"1c7fe9be","external_id":"295098a9-c956-4c70-8a57-bdfa82220574","vaultid":null,"uId":"0858ad92-321d-4b31-adbb-6307fd56efea","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>At the turn of the twentieth century, Black residents of Richmond, Virginia, had few formal options for banking and other financial services. To <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"s